WEBVTT

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.442
Shakespeare's t-shot.

00:00:11.502 --> 00:00:16.206
An idea is born.

00:00:16.266 --> 00:00:20.728
Pass the sugar, dear.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:22.800
Yes, dear.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.242
Can I have the sugar, dear?

00:00:26.302 --> 00:00:28.484
Yes, dear.

00:00:28.543 --> 00:00:30.534
Well?

00:00:30.594 --> 00:00:32.146
Sorry, dear.

00:00:32.206 --> 00:00:33.676
Oh, it's all right.

00:00:33.736 --> 00:00:37.405
I can do it myself.

00:00:37.465 --> 00:00:41.474
What are you doing today, Pete?

00:00:41.534 --> 00:00:42.544
Sorry?

00:00:42.604 --> 00:00:45.696
I said, what are you doing today?

00:00:45.716 --> 00:00:49.999
I'm not sure at the moment, dear, but don't worry.

00:00:50.019 --> 00:00:52.480
But I do worry, Pete.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.171
You have no job.

00:00:54.231 --> 00:00:55.582
We have no money.

00:00:55.642 --> 00:00:57.393
What are we going to do?

00:00:57.453 --> 00:00:59.174
Something will turn up.

00:00:59.234 --> 00:01:00.014
You see?

00:01:00.074 --> 00:01:01.875
Nothing will turn up, Pete.

00:01:01.935 --> 00:01:04.177
You just sit at home all day.

00:01:04.236 --> 00:01:05.164
Well...

00:01:05.224 --> 00:01:06.922
What do you suggest?

00:01:06.982 --> 00:01:08.504
We need to think.

00:01:08.564 --> 00:01:11.196
See what ideas we can find.

00:01:11.256 --> 00:01:13.248
Maybe I can get a job.

00:01:13.308 --> 00:01:15.370
You can't do anything, Mum.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.392
I can do lots of things.

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:19.684
Say something to our daughter, Pete.

00:01:19.744 --> 00:01:24.268
Well, let's face it, Josie does have a good point.

00:01:24.328 --> 00:01:26.122
What can you do?

00:01:26.182 --> 00:01:28.495
Thank you for your support.

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:31.316
It's obvious everyone thinks I'm stupid.

00:01:31.376 --> 00:01:35.687
But you don't have any qualifications or experience, my dear.

00:01:35.747 --> 00:01:37.847
That's because I married you.

00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:39.698
I wanted to be.

00:01:39.758 --> 00:01:42.338
I told you not to leave school, Nancy.

00:01:42.398 --> 00:01:45.269
Your father said he wasn't good enough for you.

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:48.819
We wanted you to go out with Frank from the bank.

00:01:48.879 --> 00:01:51.470
Not now, Mum, please.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.271
Your mum is getting worse.

00:01:53.331 --> 00:01:56.113
I told you we need to look for an old people's home.

00:01:56.172 --> 00:01:58.834
No, I don't want to go into it.

00:01:58.894 --> 00:02:01.876
Don't start arguing at the breakfast table.

00:02:01.896 --> 00:02:03.286
Be quiet.

00:02:03.346 --> 00:02:05.068
This isn't helping.

00:02:05.128 --> 00:02:09.230
We're trying to find some way of bringing some money into the house.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:11.691
Why not send your mother out, Nancy?

00:02:11.751 --> 00:02:16.784
The local supermarket is always looking for old people to work at night, stacking shelves.

00:02:16.844 --> 00:02:19.994
Don't be so stupid, Pete.

00:02:20.054 --> 00:02:24.254
Can you feed Boris, Josie?

00:02:24.314 --> 00:02:26.185
Mum, why do I...

00:02:26.205 --> 00:02:27.766
Just do it!

00:02:27.826 --> 00:02:28.807
Mum!

00:02:28.827 --> 00:02:29.777
Okay!

00:02:29.837 --> 00:02:31.138
I can do it.

00:02:31.198 --> 00:02:33.670
Nobody does anything in this house.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.822
Can't you say something to Josephine, Pete?

00:02:36.882 --> 00:02:41.504
Yes, dear.

00:02:41.564 --> 00:02:46.258
Fairy cakes!

00:02:46.318 --> 00:02:47.318
Sorry?

00:02:47.378 --> 00:02:50.779
Why are you talking about fairy cakes?

00:02:50.839 --> 00:02:52.700
You're good at cooking!

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.041
Why not make fairy cakes?

00:02:55.101 --> 00:02:57.162
Sell them at the church bazaar!

00:02:57.222 --> 00:02:59.284
What a car boot sale!

00:02:59.344 --> 00:03:00.654
No, Mum.

00:03:00.714 --> 00:03:03.806
I don't think that's a very good idea.

00:03:03.866 --> 00:03:05.307
Not again.

00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:07.508
How often do I tell you, Josephine?

00:03:07.568 --> 00:03:10.060
Take that rat up to your room now.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.502
But Dad is hungry.

00:03:12.562 --> 00:03:13.992
Who as I tell you?

00:03:14.012 --> 00:03:18.131
I hate you all.

00:03:18.191 --> 00:03:21.628
Wait a minute.

00:03:21.688 --> 00:03:25.572
Maybe what you said, Mum, is a good idea.

00:03:25.632 --> 00:03:26.872
Um...

00:03:26.932 --> 00:03:28.284
I know.

00:03:28.343 --> 00:03:30.335
What about a tea shop?

00:03:30.395 --> 00:03:31.856
Yes!

00:03:31.916 --> 00:03:34.718
We could open a tea shop, Mum.

00:03:34.778 --> 00:03:37.090
You and I could make the cakes.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.942
Oh, I love making cakes.

00:03:40.002 --> 00:03:43.534
My friends at the Bingo Club love my Swiss roll.

00:03:43.594 --> 00:03:46.776
That's not a bad idea, Nancy.

00:03:46.836 --> 00:03:49.300
One thing you're good at is cooking.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.104
But wait a minute.

00:03:51.164 --> 00:03:53.136
Where do we get the money?

00:03:53.195 --> 00:03:55.728
It's not cheap opening up a shop.

00:03:55.788 --> 00:03:59.270
Maybe we can get a loan from the bank.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.582
Do you think they would give us some money?

00:04:01.642 --> 00:04:02.612
Hmm.

00:04:02.672 --> 00:04:07.676
Banks always want to loan money, but first we need to write a business plan.

00:04:07.736 --> 00:04:13.580
We need to look professional and show the bank we are serious and know what we are doing.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.832
You're good at that sort of thing, Pete.

00:04:15.892 --> 00:04:16.746
Okay.

00:04:16.806 --> 00:04:18.578
I can start on it today.

00:04:18.638 --> 00:04:21.449
We need a concept.

00:04:21.509 --> 00:04:27.081
We need a detailed list of costs and our expected income.

00:04:27.141 --> 00:04:29.891
But where should we have a t-shot?

00:04:29.951 --> 00:04:32.142
Considered a competition.

00:04:32.202 --> 00:04:35.623
I think it's better if it's in a village nearby.

00:04:35.683 --> 00:04:40.947
Perhaps a traditional English tea shop in an old-fashioned village.

00:04:41.007 --> 00:04:42.739
Yes, Nancy.

00:04:42.799 --> 00:04:45.201
Oh, this is so exciting.

00:04:45.261 --> 00:04:47.633
I always wanted to have a shop.

00:04:47.693 --> 00:04:49.995
Our own tea shop.

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:53.458
Perhaps we can call it Nancy's nibbles.

00:04:53.518 --> 00:04:55.870
I don't think that's a very good name.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.143
What about Britain's best?

00:04:59.202 --> 00:05:02.049
First, let's find a shop.

00:05:02.108 --> 00:05:04.253
Switch on the computer, Pete.

00:05:04.313 --> 00:05:07.636
See if we can find an empty shop in the area.

00:05:07.696 --> 00:05:10.217
Can you go and get Josie, please, Mum?

00:05:10.277 --> 00:05:12.348
She needs to be involved as well.

00:05:12.408 --> 00:05:15.621
Of course, Nancy.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.173
We mustn't get carried away.

00:05:19.233 --> 00:05:21.674
Are you sure we're doing the right thing, Nancy?

00:05:21.734 --> 00:05:22.956
I'm a bit worried.

00:05:23.016 --> 00:05:24.677
We don't have much money.

00:05:24.736 --> 00:05:27.036
What happens if it's a disaster?

00:05:27.095 --> 00:05:29.426
It won't be a disaster.

00:05:29.486 --> 00:05:31.758
I feel it in my bones.

00:05:31.818 --> 00:05:34.351
But you know what they say in life.

00:05:34.411 --> 00:05:35.302
What's that?

00:05:35.362 --> 00:05:37.524
No risk, no fun.

00:05:37.584 --> 00:05:42.200
I just hope we're doing the right thing.

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:46.126
On the road.

00:05:46.186 --> 00:05:50.912
Hmm, there are loads of empty shops in the cotswolds.

00:05:50.972 --> 00:05:54.478
Look how much information I got at the estate agents.

00:05:54.537 --> 00:05:56.230
I like this one.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:59.724
It's modern and it's in the centre of Stratford.

00:05:59.784 --> 00:06:01.439
It looks awesome.

00:06:01.499 --> 00:06:02.963
Look at the price.

00:06:03.022 --> 00:06:04.823
It's much too expensive.

00:06:04.883 --> 00:06:06.904
What about this one?

00:06:06.964 --> 00:06:09.595
It's in Upper Snottlebrough.

00:06:09.655 --> 00:06:12.656
My friend Pierre lives in Upper Snottlebrough.

00:06:12.716 --> 00:06:13.537
Nice man.

00:06:13.597 --> 00:06:15.418
He comes from Germany, I think.

00:06:15.458 --> 00:06:16.428
I'm not sure.

00:06:16.488 --> 00:06:18.779
He doesn't speak much English.

00:06:18.839 --> 00:06:20.430
Nice eyes.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.761
He told me he puts garlic on his warts.

00:06:23.821 --> 00:06:25.972
I think that's what he said.

00:06:26.032 --> 00:06:28.920
Are you sure he's German, Mum?

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:30.999
Oh, here, look.

00:06:31.058 --> 00:06:32.990
Why a piddle?

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:35.442
That village is only down the road.

00:06:35.502 --> 00:06:37.494
Look at the picture.

00:06:37.554 --> 00:06:43.865
The price is a bit high, but maybe we can speak to the owner and get it reduced a bit.

00:06:43.925 --> 00:06:46.171
It looks as if it's going to collapse.

00:06:46.231 --> 00:06:47.752
It looks interesting.

00:06:47.812 --> 00:06:49.733
Shall we go and look at it now?

00:06:49.793 --> 00:06:51.004
Good idea.

00:06:51.064 --> 00:06:53.445
Go and get the car out of the garage, dear.

00:06:53.465 --> 00:06:54.776
All right.

00:06:54.836 --> 00:06:57.668
Ooh, this is so exciting.

00:06:57.728 --> 00:06:59.739
I want to put on some makeup.

00:06:59.799 --> 00:07:02.821
We're not meeting royalty, mum.

00:07:02.881 --> 00:07:12.078
Come on, Josie, and bring Boris with you.

00:07:12.138 --> 00:07:20.886
What is wrong with this car?

00:07:20.946 --> 00:07:22.357
Piece of junk.

00:07:22.417 --> 00:07:25.390
We're going very slowly.

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:27.772
I can walk quicker.

00:07:27.832 --> 00:07:29.434
Then why don't you?

00:07:29.494 --> 00:07:31.866
What's he saying about me?

00:07:31.926 --> 00:07:34.528
He knows I can't hear very well.

00:07:34.588 --> 00:07:36.580
Be quiet, both of you.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:37.780
Now.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.391
What could we call a tea shop?

00:07:41.451 --> 00:07:44.333
Time for tea, perhaps?

00:07:44.393 --> 00:07:46.945
Or truly scrumptious?

00:07:47.005 --> 00:07:48.076
Yes.

00:07:48.136 --> 00:07:49.387
They're OK.

00:07:49.407 --> 00:07:53.410
I'm sure this car's all right.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:54.771
Of course it is.

00:07:54.831 --> 00:07:55.702
It's just...

00:07:55.762 --> 00:07:58.164
We're going slower and slower.

00:07:58.224 --> 00:07:58.994
What is...

00:07:59.054 --> 00:08:00.806
It's OK.

00:08:00.866 --> 00:08:03.228
The car is a bit full today.

00:08:03.288 --> 00:08:08.124
Perhaps eating two pieces of toast for breakfast was a bad idea.

00:08:08.184 --> 00:08:10.962
Are you talking about Nancy?

00:08:11.022 --> 00:08:12.094
Oh!

00:08:12.154 --> 00:08:14.576
Dad!

00:08:14.636 --> 00:08:16.178
Yes, Josie!

00:08:16.238 --> 00:08:18.820
I hope you don't mind me mentioning it, but...

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:20.531
Yes, yes, what is it?

00:08:20.591 --> 00:08:23.662
Well, our next door neighbour has just overtaken us.

00:08:23.722 --> 00:08:24.462
So?

00:08:24.522 --> 00:08:26.923
He was on his bike!

00:08:26.983 --> 00:08:31.985
Yes, that's right, laugh, I don't mind.

00:08:32.045 --> 00:08:33.956
Oh, no, look!

00:08:34.016 --> 00:08:35.167
What is it, Mum?

00:08:35.226 --> 00:08:36.347
What's the matter?

00:08:36.407 --> 00:08:38.868
A hill in front of us!

00:08:38.928 --> 00:08:40.429
What happens now?

00:08:40.489 --> 00:08:41.149
Help!

00:08:41.209 --> 00:08:44.424
A hill!

00:08:44.484 --> 00:08:49.872
We need a theme for the shop.

00:08:49.932 --> 00:08:51.312
Good idea.

00:08:51.372 --> 00:08:53.714
Something that's a bit different.

00:08:53.774 --> 00:08:57.115
We can all put on fancy dress clothes.

00:08:57.175 --> 00:08:58.326
No, mum.

00:08:58.386 --> 00:09:00.076
Yes, of course.

00:09:00.137 --> 00:09:01.708
Why not?

00:09:01.768 --> 00:09:06.070
Everyone can dress up in Elizabethan costumes.

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:08.931
You're not serious, are you, Josie?

00:09:08.991 --> 00:09:11.903
Why Elizabethan costumes?

00:09:11.963 --> 00:09:13.784
It's obvious, mum.

00:09:13.844 --> 00:09:16.475
I want to be Lady Di.

00:09:16.535 --> 00:09:18.917
She was so beautiful.

00:09:18.977 --> 00:09:20.098
What a pity!

00:09:20.158 --> 00:09:24.132
I think your grandmother's a bit confused with her history.

00:09:24.192 --> 00:09:25.674
No mum.

00:09:25.734 --> 00:09:28.977
Elizabethan is the time of Elizabeth the first.

00:09:29.037 --> 00:09:32.050
I'm not talking about Elizabeth the second.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:35.874
Elizabeth the first was in 1716.

00:09:35.934 --> 00:09:39.536
Well, a long time ago.

00:09:39.556 --> 00:09:42.788
But anyway, I don't understand.

00:09:42.848 --> 00:09:45.019
Why Elizabethan, Josie?

00:09:45.079 --> 00:09:48.830
Well, my piddle is very near Stratford.

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:49.521
So?

00:09:49.541 --> 00:09:51.934
Oh, Mom.

00:09:51.994 --> 00:09:55.148
Did you ever go to school?

00:09:55.208 --> 00:09:59.682
Shakespeare, William Shakespeare was born there.

00:09:59.742 --> 00:10:02.824
Oh yes, of course.

00:10:02.884 --> 00:10:04.154
Yes.

00:10:04.214 --> 00:10:08.057
We can call the tea shop the Shakespeare tea shop.

00:10:08.117 --> 00:10:13.891
Oh no, better is the only Shakespeare tea shopping.

00:10:13.951 --> 00:10:16.222
It sounds older and more traditional.

00:10:16.282 --> 00:10:22.603
We can then dress up in clothes from Shakespeare's time and attract tourists.

00:10:22.663 --> 00:10:29.362
Dad, can dress up in a pair of tights in the shop to look like William Shakespeare.

00:10:29.422 --> 00:10:30.342
What did you say?

00:10:30.362 --> 00:10:32.412
Oh, don't worry Pete.

00:10:32.472 --> 00:10:39.594
Josephine didn't say anything important.

00:10:39.654 --> 00:10:44.465
Looking at the tea shop.

00:10:44.525 --> 00:10:47.315
It's very quiet here.

00:10:47.376 --> 00:10:49.586
Is anyone alive here?

00:10:49.606 --> 00:10:53.040
Oh, it's a lovely village.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:56.272
Lots of wonderful old buildings.

00:10:56.332 --> 00:10:58.534
Oh, there's the village pub.

00:10:58.594 --> 00:11:01.446
And look, an old church.

00:11:01.506 --> 00:11:03.358
It's really nice here.

00:11:03.418 --> 00:11:05.319
It's so peaceful.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:08.702
There's more life in a funeral procession.

00:11:08.722 --> 00:11:10.924
Look, there it is.

00:11:10.984 --> 00:11:13.006
Isn't it cute?

00:11:13.066 --> 00:11:14.477
Is that it?

00:11:14.537 --> 00:11:16.664
Next to the village shop?

00:11:16.724 --> 00:11:18.932
God, it's awful!

00:11:18.992 --> 00:11:21.054
Oh, it's lovely!

00:11:21.114 --> 00:11:23.216
Lots of wooden beams!

00:11:23.276 --> 00:11:25.778
It needs a lot of work doing on it.

00:11:25.838 --> 00:11:27.569
We have to lower the price.

00:11:27.629 --> 00:11:30.101
The bank won't give us enough money from this place.

00:11:30.161 --> 00:11:32.634
Don't be so negative, Pete.

00:11:32.694 --> 00:11:35.236
We can ask in the village shop next door.

00:11:35.296 --> 00:11:37.068
Perhaps they know the owner.

00:11:37.127 --> 00:11:42.169
Come on!

00:11:42.229 --> 00:11:47.401
Good morning.

00:11:47.421 --> 00:11:49.863
I wonder if you can help us.

00:11:49.924 --> 00:11:51.736
I certainly hope I can.

00:11:51.796 --> 00:11:54.248
What an enchanting blouse.

00:11:54.308 --> 00:11:55.530
It looks new.

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.191
Silk, isn't it?

00:11:57.251 --> 00:11:58.613
Yes, it is.

00:11:58.672 --> 00:12:00.584
And thank you.

00:12:00.644 --> 00:12:05.616
My name is Nancy Britain and this is my husband, Pete.

00:12:05.676 --> 00:12:09.616
And a lovely little doggy-woggy!

00:12:09.676 --> 00:12:11.899
Doggy-woggy!

00:12:11.959 --> 00:12:13.711
What's a doggy-woggy?

00:12:13.771 --> 00:12:18.461
And this is my daughter, Josephine, and my mother!

00:12:18.521 --> 00:12:21.167
It's so enchanting to meet you all.

00:12:21.227 --> 00:12:22.558
What can I do for you?

00:12:22.618 --> 00:12:27.021
Well, my husband and I are interested in the shop next door.

00:12:27.081 --> 00:12:29.313
We're thinking of opening a tea shop.

00:12:29.373 --> 00:12:31.184
How enchanting.

00:12:31.244 --> 00:12:32.706
New neighbours.

00:12:32.766 --> 00:12:34.547
Come on, I have a key.

00:12:34.607 --> 00:12:36.768
Oh, how terrible of me.

00:12:36.828 --> 00:12:38.900
I didn't introduce myself.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:40.271
I'm Simon.

00:12:40.331 --> 00:12:41.392
Call me Si.

00:12:41.452 --> 00:12:42.466
Everyone does.

00:12:42.526 --> 00:12:47.096
Julian Deere, look after the shop, back in two shakes of a lamb's tail.

00:12:47.156 --> 00:12:48.680
Come on, come on.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:56.122
We can look together.

00:12:56.182 --> 00:13:04.644
What a wonderful shop.

00:13:04.704 --> 00:13:06.115
It's perfect.

00:13:06.175 --> 00:13:07.195
God.

00:13:07.255 --> 00:13:09.816
Who lived here Henry VIII?

00:13:09.877 --> 00:13:12.918
Does it have electricity and running water?

00:13:12.978 --> 00:13:14.629
Of course it does.

00:13:14.689 --> 00:13:16.320
It's too expensive.

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:17.500
I need the loo.

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:18.481
Oh, wait for me.

00:13:18.501 --> 00:13:20.402
I do too.

00:13:20.462 --> 00:13:22.903
There's a lot of work to do.

00:13:22.923 --> 00:13:25.304
It looks rather run down.

00:13:25.324 --> 00:13:28.626
It's very old-fashioned.

00:13:28.685 --> 00:13:31.741
Even older than your mother, if that's possible.

00:13:31.801 --> 00:13:33.516
You're so funny.

00:13:33.536 --> 00:13:35.587
I wasn't trying to be funny.

00:13:35.647 --> 00:13:37.028
Oh, I see.

00:13:37.088 --> 00:13:40.010
I can see we're going to have such fun together.

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:41.490
Yes.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:43.282
Is there a kitchen?

00:13:43.342 --> 00:13:44.343
Of course.

00:13:44.403 --> 00:13:47.214
Julian and I always kept it spotless.

00:13:47.274 --> 00:13:49.726
We're real little housewives together.

00:13:49.786 --> 00:13:51.598
I don't understand.

00:13:51.658 --> 00:13:52.938
Oh, no, of course.

00:13:52.998 --> 00:13:55.060
Julian and I own this shop.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:58.202
We moved our village shop next door because it's bigger.

00:13:58.262 --> 00:14:00.714
So now we want to rent this shop.

00:14:00.774 --> 00:14:03.306
The toilets in the garden!

00:14:03.366 --> 00:14:05.507
It's an outside love!

00:14:05.567 --> 00:14:07.308
Oh, it's like the Middle Ages.

00:14:07.368 --> 00:14:09.529
It's so wonderful!

00:14:09.589 --> 00:14:11.580
Reminds you of your childhood, does it?

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:13.501
Oh, there you go again.

00:14:13.561 --> 00:14:15.042
Your husband's so funny.

00:14:15.062 --> 00:14:18.194
We're going to get on like a house on fire.

00:14:18.254 --> 00:14:20.805
I'm going back to my shop.

00:14:20.865 --> 00:14:23.276
Bring the key when you finish looking round.

00:14:23.336 --> 00:14:25.317
Bye!

00:14:25.377 --> 00:14:29.005
Well, what do you think?

00:14:29.065 --> 00:14:31.242
I'm very happy I'm married to you.

00:14:31.302 --> 00:14:32.893
Oh, I mean the shop.

00:14:32.953 --> 00:14:34.514
What do you think?

00:14:34.574 --> 00:14:36.284
It's a lot of work.

00:14:36.344 --> 00:14:37.655
I'm a bit worried.

00:14:37.715 --> 00:14:39.416
It's wonderful.

00:14:39.476 --> 00:14:42.688
We must all help and that includes you, Josie.

00:14:42.748 --> 00:14:44.258
Of course, Mum.

00:14:44.318 --> 00:14:46.690
Have you told Dad about the toys?

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:47.800
Sorry.

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:48.771
What did you say?

00:14:48.791 --> 00:14:50.011
Tights.

00:14:50.031 --> 00:14:50.692
Don't worry.

00:14:50.752 --> 00:14:52.062
You don't have to.

00:14:52.122 --> 00:14:53.953
Don't have to what?

00:14:54.013 --> 00:14:54.934
Come on then.

00:14:54.994 --> 00:14:57.792
Let's take the key back to Julian and Sai.

00:14:57.852 --> 00:14:59.819
I want to have a word with them.

00:14:59.879 --> 00:15:01.409
It's much too expensive.

00:15:01.469 --> 00:15:03.910
There's so much to renovate.

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:05.541
What's this about?

00:15:05.601 --> 00:15:06.762
Tights, Josie.

00:15:06.822 --> 00:15:09.213
Don't tell Junian inside.

00:15:09.273 --> 00:15:12.804
They'll get excited.

00:15:12.864 --> 00:15:18.418
What are you all talking about?

00:15:18.478 --> 00:15:21.199
Oh hello again.

00:15:21.259 --> 00:15:22.930
Well, what do you think?

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:24.992
We really like it.

00:15:25.052 --> 00:15:27.373
It's just what we're looking for.

00:15:27.434 --> 00:15:33.488
I think it's a bit expensive and then there's the deposit as well.

00:15:33.548 --> 00:15:35.650
And it needs a lot of renovation.

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:37.111
We'll have to do it ourselves.

00:15:37.171 --> 00:15:39.032
I know, I know.

00:15:39.092 --> 00:15:43.085
Why don't we all meet next week at the estate agents office?

00:15:43.145 --> 00:15:46.237
We can then discuss the money and the contract together.

00:15:46.297 --> 00:15:47.698
I'm sure we can agree.

00:15:47.758 --> 00:15:48.530
What do you think?

00:15:48.589 --> 00:15:49.420
Yes.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:50.990
That's a good idea.

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:53.961
Here's our name and phone number.

00:15:54.021 --> 00:15:56.582
Just ring us up to tell us when the meeting is.

00:15:56.642 --> 00:15:57.873
Wonderful.

00:15:57.932 --> 00:15:59.493
Thank you so much.

00:15:59.553 --> 00:16:00.963
Come on, everyone.

00:16:01.023 --> 00:16:04.004
Let's go and look around the village and meet the locals.

00:16:04.064 --> 00:16:05.715
Not so fast, dear.

00:16:05.775 --> 00:16:07.155
One step at a time.

00:16:07.215 --> 00:16:09.506
A lot of things can go wrong, you know.

00:16:09.526 --> 00:16:12.006
Don't talk such rubbish, Pete.

00:16:12.046 --> 00:16:14.697
Come on, everyone.

00:16:14.757 --> 00:16:16.758
Women.

00:16:16.818 --> 00:16:19.506
I know what you mean.

00:16:19.566 --> 00:16:23.436
Setting up the tea shop.

00:16:23.496 --> 00:16:26.620
Don't let your father see that you have Robbie with you.

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:28.142
You know he hates it.

00:16:28.202 --> 00:16:30.344
I want him to see the tea shop.

00:16:30.404 --> 00:16:32.936
You want to see a dummy Robbie?

00:16:32.996 --> 00:16:35.235
If it isn't, please, Josie.

00:16:35.295 --> 00:16:37.586
You need to put it on a diet.

00:16:37.646 --> 00:16:39.426
He's not fat.

00:16:39.486 --> 00:16:41.016
Come here, little Robbie.

00:16:41.076 --> 00:16:42.057
I still love you.

00:16:42.077 --> 00:16:46.687
I love the old tables and chairs you found, Nancy.

00:16:46.747 --> 00:16:47.538
Yes.

00:16:47.598 --> 00:16:49.638
Ebay is a wonderful thing.

00:16:49.698 --> 00:16:50.558
B-day.

00:16:50.578 --> 00:16:52.788
I forgot one of those as well.

00:16:52.848 --> 00:16:53.758
Sorry, mother.

00:16:53.818 --> 00:16:58.609
My friend Pierre in Upper Snottlebrough has one in his bathroom.

00:16:58.669 --> 00:16:59.600
I saw it.

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:01.830
He washes his socks in it.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:03.682
I think that's what he said.

00:17:03.722 --> 00:17:04.593
I'm not sure.

00:17:04.653 --> 00:17:06.064
I don't speak German.

00:17:06.124 --> 00:17:07.736
No, I said...

00:17:07.796 --> 00:17:09.348
Oh, it doesn't matter.

00:17:09.408 --> 00:17:11.960
Are you sure he's German, Mum?

00:17:12.021 --> 00:17:14.053
If his name's Pierre, then he's...

00:17:14.113 --> 00:17:15.134
I think so.

00:17:15.154 --> 00:17:16.936
He looks German.

00:17:16.996 --> 00:17:20.588
What's that noise?

00:17:20.648 --> 00:17:26.400
Oh yes, it's the shop sign company.

00:17:26.461 --> 00:17:29.092
They're putting up the sign on the front of the shop.

00:17:29.152 --> 00:17:31.014
It's so exciting!

00:17:31.074 --> 00:17:34.298
Our own t-shop!

00:17:34.358 --> 00:17:40.522
Mum, can you and Josie start to think about what to write on the menus?

00:17:40.582 --> 00:17:43.924
Oh, and can you keep an eye on the men outside?

00:17:43.984 --> 00:17:47.536
Check they don't put the shop sign upside down.

00:17:47.596 --> 00:17:50.388
Of course, leave it to us.

00:17:50.448 --> 00:17:57.362
If this shop's going to have a Shakespeare theme, then I suppose the food should have a Shakespeare name too.

00:17:57.422 --> 00:17:59.703
That's a good idea Josie.

00:17:59.763 --> 00:18:01.307
What do you suggest?

00:18:01.367 --> 00:18:06.496
What about, um, Yorick Yorkshire jam cake?

00:18:06.556 --> 00:18:10.792
Or, uh, Macbeth Marang Pie?

00:18:10.852 --> 00:18:12.163
That's good.

00:18:12.183 --> 00:18:16.769
How about Stephen Scones?

00:18:16.829 --> 00:18:18.584
Sorry, Grant.

00:18:18.644 --> 00:18:20.338
I don't understand.

00:18:20.398 --> 00:18:22.159
Why Steven scones?

00:18:22.219 --> 00:18:24.711
He's a good friend at the Bingo Club.

00:18:24.771 --> 00:18:28.364
Yes, but there has to be a connection with Shakespeare.

00:18:28.424 --> 00:18:29.906
Yes, there is.

00:18:29.966 --> 00:18:34.839
Steven took me in his car to the Globe Theatre in London.

00:18:34.899 --> 00:18:36.650
It's where Shakespeare performs.

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:41.844
Yes, I know what it is, but no one here knows about Steven.

00:18:41.904 --> 00:18:43.956
What do you want, Pete?

00:18:44.016 --> 00:18:46.629
Do you think we're making the right decision?

00:18:46.689 --> 00:18:49.130
I'm not now, Pete.

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:51.411
We both signed the contract.

00:18:51.471 --> 00:18:56.062
The opening party is on Saturday and there's so much to do and now you...

00:18:56.122 --> 00:18:58.282
I'm worried about the money.

00:18:58.342 --> 00:19:01.603
I just hope we get enough customers to pay the rent.

00:19:01.663 --> 00:19:04.613
And the deposit was bigger than I thought it was going to be.

00:19:04.673 --> 00:19:05.733
Stop it, Pete.

00:19:05.794 --> 00:19:07.244
Don't go on so.

00:19:07.304 --> 00:19:08.584
Did you see the interest?

00:19:08.604 --> 00:19:09.974
We have to pay the bank.

00:19:10.034 --> 00:19:13.312
Now I know where the bank manager drives a Mercedes.

00:19:13.372 --> 00:19:15.921
And all the money we spent on renovation.

00:19:15.961 --> 00:19:18.454
Yes, yes, anyway.

00:19:18.514 --> 00:19:20.667
What did you call me for?

00:19:20.727 --> 00:19:23.100
Is this where you want the shelves?

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:28.060
Yes, that's fine.

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:32.198
What happened?

00:19:32.258 --> 00:19:34.239
Pete, are you alright?

00:19:34.299 --> 00:19:36.991
Yes, but these walls are not very thick.

00:19:37.011 --> 00:19:39.423
Hello, what a shark!

00:19:39.483 --> 00:19:42.655
A Julian, a hole in our lovely wall!

00:19:42.716 --> 00:19:44.176
Hello, Simon.

00:19:44.236 --> 00:19:45.607
I mean, Si.

00:19:45.668 --> 00:19:46.948
I'm sorry, but...

00:19:47.008 --> 00:19:51.994
My heart stopped for a second, trying to spy on us, were you?

00:19:52.054 --> 00:19:54.345
And no, of course not.

00:19:54.405 --> 00:19:56.226
I'm sorry about the wall.

00:19:56.286 --> 00:19:57.406
I can repair it.

00:19:57.426 --> 00:19:58.266
That's okay.

00:19:58.326 --> 00:19:59.496
It's rather fun.

00:19:59.556 --> 00:20:00.867
We can talk to each other.

00:20:00.887 --> 00:20:02.437
Eat.

00:20:02.497 --> 00:20:04.957
You're making more work for us.

00:20:05.017 --> 00:20:06.648
We open on Saturday.

00:20:06.668 --> 00:20:09.438
We still have so much to do.

00:20:09.498 --> 00:20:11.589
I want to do some painting.

00:20:11.649 --> 00:20:12.849
Yes, dear.

00:20:12.909 --> 00:20:15.249
Can you get me the ladder and the paint, please?

00:20:15.309 --> 00:20:16.130
They're in the kitchen.

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:20.152
Yes, dear.

00:20:20.212 --> 00:20:24.416
Where do you want me to put...

00:20:24.436 --> 00:20:28.558
Be careful, Boris is like...

00:20:28.618 --> 00:20:31.680
Oh my god!

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:33.462
Look at Boris!

00:20:33.522 --> 00:20:36.854
He's covered in paint and the floor!

00:20:36.914 --> 00:20:38.294
Look at the floor!

00:20:38.355 --> 00:20:40.986
How stupid you are!

00:20:41.047 --> 00:20:46.280
I'm going outside and you can clean everything up before I get back!

00:20:46.340 --> 00:20:48.692
Yes dear.

00:20:48.752 --> 00:20:50.082
Pete?

00:20:50.142 --> 00:20:51.490
Come here!

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:53.288
Where are the workmen?

00:20:53.348 --> 00:20:54.669
Gone home already?

00:20:54.689 --> 00:20:56.121
Yes, dear.

00:20:56.181 --> 00:20:57.231
Is there a problem?

00:20:57.292 --> 00:21:00.985
Why did I let you organise the sign?

00:21:01.045 --> 00:21:03.417
Now I know why they were so cheap!

00:21:03.477 --> 00:21:05.399
You need to save money!

00:21:05.419 --> 00:21:09.212
It looks very nice to...

00:21:09.272 --> 00:21:10.654
me.

00:21:10.714 --> 00:21:11.974
Mom!

00:21:12.034 --> 00:21:13.456
Josephine!

00:21:13.516 --> 00:21:15.401
Come here!

00:21:15.461 --> 00:21:18.737
Why didn't you keep an eye on them?

00:21:18.757 --> 00:21:20.509
We can't do everything.

00:21:20.569 --> 00:21:24.062
I like the colours so bright and cheerful.

00:21:24.082 --> 00:21:27.715
I don't think mum is angry about the colours, Gran.

00:21:27.775 --> 00:21:29.567
What's the problem?

00:21:29.626 --> 00:21:31.959
Oh, I see now.

00:21:32.019 --> 00:21:33.850
See, me.

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:36.022
It's the spelling.

00:21:36.082 --> 00:21:38.264
It's not funny, mum.

00:21:38.325 --> 00:21:39.856
Phone them now, Pete.

00:21:39.916 --> 00:21:40.857
This moment.

00:21:40.917 --> 00:21:42.373
Here's my mobile.

00:21:42.433 --> 00:21:43.558
Yes, sir.

00:21:43.618 --> 00:21:45.240
Perhaps I will.

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:47.271
I'm sure there's a good reason.

00:21:47.291 --> 00:21:47.812
Yes.

00:21:47.872 --> 00:21:51.204
Stupidity is the only possible reason.

00:21:51.264 --> 00:21:56.187
I want it changed.

00:21:56.247 --> 00:22:01.230
Pete Britain speaking.

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:03.542
The old Shakespeare t-shop.

00:22:03.602 --> 00:22:05.363
Why a piddle?

00:22:05.423 --> 00:22:08.140
No, I don't like it.

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:09.635
Why not?

00:22:09.695 --> 00:22:11.046
Why not?

00:22:11.106 --> 00:22:13.826
I want to tell you why not.

00:22:13.886 --> 00:22:17.117
No, the colours are fine.

00:22:17.177 --> 00:22:20.157
Yes, it's in the right place.

00:22:20.217 --> 00:22:21.578
Yes.

00:22:21.638 --> 00:22:23.428
And it's big enough.

00:22:23.488 --> 00:22:26.869
The problem is the words.

00:22:26.929 --> 00:22:34.220
Correct me if I'm wrong, but I think there is a letter P in the word Shakespeare.

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:36.611
What do you mean am I sure?

00:22:36.671 --> 00:22:38.548
Of course I'm sure.

00:22:38.608 --> 00:22:40.375
What?

00:22:40.435 --> 00:22:43.806
You hoped I wouldn't notice.

00:22:43.866 --> 00:22:46.107
Of course I noticed.

00:22:46.167 --> 00:22:52.548
Anyone with any intelligence can see that Shakespeare has P, P, P in the middle.

00:22:52.608 --> 00:22:59.180
We can't have the name the old Shakespeare T-Shop, can we?

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:02.530
No one understands that.

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:05.051
What do you say?

00:23:05.111 --> 00:23:07.602
I don't believe it.

00:23:07.662 --> 00:23:08.908
What's the reason?

00:23:08.969 --> 00:23:12.949
He says he didn't have enough letter P's to put on the sign.

00:23:13.009 --> 00:23:14.570
What are you talking about?

00:23:14.630 --> 00:23:16.472
Give the phone to me.

00:23:16.532 --> 00:23:18.844
This is Mrs Britain speaking.

00:23:18.904 --> 00:23:20.296
Sorry?

00:23:20.356 --> 00:23:23.589
I don't want to hear any more excuses.

00:23:23.649 --> 00:23:27.723
I want to have a P on our sign by the end of the afternoon.

00:23:27.783 --> 00:23:28.854
Goodbye!

00:23:28.914 --> 00:23:32.157
Do you know what you just said, Mom?

00:23:32.217 --> 00:23:32.954
Sorry?

00:23:33.014 --> 00:23:36.892
This is not the best moment to start laughing, Josephine.

00:23:36.952 --> 00:23:38.113
Stop it.

00:23:38.133 --> 00:23:39.684
Stop it, Josie.

00:23:39.744 --> 00:23:41.895
Oh, what's so funny?

00:23:41.915 --> 00:23:44.166
I don't understand.

00:23:44.226 --> 00:23:48.410
Mom said she wanted to have a pee on our side.

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:51.481
You were all completely mad.

00:23:51.541 --> 00:23:58.996
I'm going inside.

00:23:59.056 --> 00:24:01.741
The housewarming party.

00:24:01.801 --> 00:24:04.976
Is everything ready for the big opening?

00:24:05.036 --> 00:24:06.307
Yes, Nancy.

00:24:06.367 --> 00:24:07.197
Don't worry.

00:24:07.217 --> 00:24:08.807
I do worry.

00:24:08.867 --> 00:24:11.098
I'm sure there's something we've forgotten.

00:24:11.118 --> 00:24:12.818
Stay cool, Mum.

00:24:12.858 --> 00:24:14.238
Everything's OK.

00:24:14.298 --> 00:24:16.509
Did you deliver all the invitations?

00:24:16.569 --> 00:24:19.340
Josie and I did it last week, Nancy.

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:20.910
The local paper.

00:24:20.970 --> 00:24:22.420
I forgot to phone Mum.

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:23.740
I did it, Mum.

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:25.681
They'll be here in a minute.

00:24:25.741 --> 00:24:28.262
Everything looks alright in the shop.

00:24:28.322 --> 00:24:30.912
Oh God, it's almost 10 o'clock!

00:24:30.972 --> 00:24:33.142
I love the dress is Nancy.

00:24:33.202 --> 00:24:34.833
I feel like a queen.

00:24:34.893 --> 00:24:37.563
You look great too, Dad.

00:24:37.623 --> 00:24:40.424
I can't be a waiter in the shop wearing these.

00:24:40.484 --> 00:24:42.484
What are the customers going to say?

00:24:42.544 --> 00:24:43.904
Come on, Pete.

00:24:43.964 --> 00:24:45.624
No one will notice.

00:24:45.684 --> 00:24:47.265
No one will notice.

00:24:47.325 --> 00:24:49.295
Of course people will notice.

00:24:49.355 --> 00:24:52.266
Everyone will see I'm wearing black tights.

00:24:52.326 --> 00:24:54.310
It's too embarrassing.

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:58.748
They are very tight in some places.

00:24:58.808 --> 00:25:00.600
And this high colour.

00:25:00.660 --> 00:25:01.790
I can't look down.

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:04.122
I can't see what I'm carrying.

00:25:04.183 --> 00:25:06.044
Just leave them on.

00:25:06.104 --> 00:25:07.646
You look...

00:25:07.706 --> 00:25:09.577
lovely.

00:25:09.638 --> 00:25:12.163
Oh, no music.

00:25:12.223 --> 00:25:13.216
Sorry, Nancy.

00:25:13.277 --> 00:25:14.897
We need some background music.

00:25:14.957 --> 00:25:16.358
Oh, that's all right.

00:25:16.418 --> 00:25:19.480
I can sing some songs from the Bingo Club.

00:25:19.500 --> 00:25:24.172
Old back door, old head of heart.

00:25:24.232 --> 00:25:26.753
We don't want complaints from the neighbours.

00:25:26.813 --> 00:25:28.354
No, thank you mum.

00:25:28.414 --> 00:25:31.953
It's not necessary.

00:25:32.014 --> 00:25:36.654
Hello, Si, but what?

00:25:36.714 --> 00:25:42.809
These are the wire-piddle-morris men.

00:25:42.869 --> 00:25:48.053
Julian and I are in the village dance group.

00:25:48.113 --> 00:25:52.367
Thought it would be nice to dance outside the tea shop when all the guests arrive.

00:25:52.427 --> 00:25:54.058
Oh, how exciting!

00:25:54.118 --> 00:25:56.490
Oh, what a super idea!

00:25:56.550 --> 00:25:58.472
Thank you, Si.

00:25:58.532 --> 00:26:00.593
Oh, thank you Nancy!

00:26:00.653 --> 00:26:04.686
Come on then, outside, and let's start dancing!

00:26:04.746 --> 00:26:09.018
What a cool idea!

00:26:09.078 --> 00:26:13.691
First guests are here!

00:26:13.751 --> 00:26:15.352
Come on, everyone!

00:26:15.412 --> 00:26:18.564
This is tremendous, congratulations.

00:26:18.624 --> 00:26:20.275
Lovely to see you.

00:26:20.335 --> 00:26:21.475
Thank you very much.

00:26:21.495 --> 00:26:24.307
Please help yourselves to sandwiches and cakes.

00:26:24.367 --> 00:26:27.368
My husband's over there if you'd like a cup of tea.

00:26:27.428 --> 00:26:30.498
Yes, that's right, the one in the tide.

00:26:30.558 --> 00:26:33.289
Yes, yes, sir, be madam.

00:26:33.349 --> 00:26:39.300
If you could just wait a minute, sorry, I thought you said Darjeeling, sir.

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:41.101
No, I'd see.

00:26:41.161 --> 00:26:42.981
You wanted a Sam.

00:26:43.041 --> 00:26:43.702
Yes?

00:26:43.762 --> 00:26:45.252
Yes, I'm doing my best.

00:26:45.312 --> 00:26:47.532
Don't get your knickers in a twist.

00:26:47.592 --> 00:26:49.963
No, I didn't say anything, sir.

00:26:50.023 --> 00:26:53.228
Just contemplating the suicide.

00:26:53.288 --> 00:26:56.356
Is there perhaps anyone who could help me?

00:26:56.416 --> 00:26:59.478
Nancy, Josie?

00:26:59.538 --> 00:27:01.770
Thought not.

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:02.782
What?

00:27:02.842 --> 00:27:03.522
What?

00:27:03.582 --> 00:27:05.444
Yes, of course it's tea.

00:27:05.504 --> 00:27:08.690
This is a tea shop, madam.

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:12.633
Good morning, my name's Michael Trumpleton from The Stratford.

00:27:12.653 --> 00:27:17.405
Yes, I'm my name's Pete Britton, and that over there is my lovely domineering wife.

00:27:17.465 --> 00:27:19.806
And the laughing high eener is her mother.

00:27:19.866 --> 00:27:21.056
Can't you see I'm busy?

00:27:21.116 --> 00:27:22.387
You don't understand.

00:27:22.447 --> 00:27:24.068
Can you wake your turn, please?

00:27:24.128 --> 00:27:25.768
There are others in front of you.

00:27:25.828 --> 00:27:28.159
The younger generation too.

00:27:28.219 --> 00:27:30.070
You are madam, two teas.

00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:31.150
Sorry?

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:32.681
What did you want?

00:27:32.741 --> 00:27:37.858
Two coffees.

00:27:37.918 --> 00:27:42.998
Excuse me, Mrs Britain.

00:27:43.038 --> 00:27:45.860
My name's Michael Trumpleton from the Stratford Gazette.

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:48.473
Oh, the newspaper.

00:27:48.533 --> 00:27:49.384
Oh, yes.

00:27:49.444 --> 00:27:50.745
How lovely to meet you.

00:27:50.765 --> 00:27:53.318
Oh, please call me Nancy.

00:27:53.378 --> 00:27:54.209
Oh, yes.

00:27:54.269 --> 00:27:55.860
How lovely to meet you.

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:57.262
Nice to meet you, Nancy.

00:27:57.322 --> 00:28:00.655
Come sit down here and my husband will get you a pot of tea.

00:28:00.715 --> 00:28:02.594
Help yourself to some biscuits.

00:28:02.654 --> 00:28:08.140
If your husband's the man in the rather uncomfortable tights, then I think he's rather busy at the moment.

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:09.522
So you've met him already?

00:28:09.542 --> 00:28:09.782
Yes!

00:28:09.842 --> 00:28:14.208
He likes them like that!

00:28:14.268 --> 00:28:17.052
Oh wow!

00:28:17.112 --> 00:28:19.440
These are super!

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:22.136
Better than my mum's cooking.

00:28:22.196 --> 00:28:23.877
No, thank you.

00:28:23.938 --> 00:28:25.929
It's my own recipe.

00:28:25.989 --> 00:28:28.791
Perhaps you could give me a list of the ingredients.

00:28:28.851 --> 00:28:31.553
It would be good to put it into the newspaper article.

00:28:31.613 --> 00:28:33.254
Yes, of course.

00:28:33.314 --> 00:28:35.276
It's very simple really.

00:28:35.336 --> 00:28:40.480
Just flour, butter, nutmeg, some milk and eggs.

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:41.851
I think that's all.

00:28:41.911 --> 00:28:43.893
Oh yes, and lemon juice.

00:28:43.953 --> 00:28:44.673
Thank you.

00:28:44.734 --> 00:28:49.377
I know you're all very busy at the moment but could I have a photo of you all in front of the shop?

00:28:49.437 --> 00:28:50.968
Of course you can!

00:28:50.988 --> 00:28:52.230
Mum!

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:53.330
Josie!

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:54.311
Pete!

00:28:54.371 --> 00:28:56.262
Can you come here a moment please?

00:28:56.322 --> 00:28:58.964
The newspaper wants a photo of us!

00:28:59.024 --> 00:29:00.096
Coming!

00:29:00.156 --> 00:29:00.756
Ooh!

00:29:00.816 --> 00:29:02.488
How exciting!

00:29:02.548 --> 00:29:03.578
What now?

00:29:03.638 --> 00:29:06.500
Yes, now please Pete!

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:08.592
What a life!

00:29:08.653 --> 00:29:10.614
Just help yourself everyone!

00:29:10.674 --> 00:29:12.991
Be careful with the kettle, it's very hot!

00:29:13.052 --> 00:29:15.488
wouldn't want you to burn yourself?

00:29:15.548 --> 00:29:18.121
Yes, Madame, that is Darjeeling tea.

00:29:18.181 --> 00:29:19.702
It's written on the packet.

00:29:19.762 --> 00:29:21.073
D-A-R.

00:29:21.133 --> 00:29:22.224
Heat!

00:29:22.284 --> 00:29:28.864
I'm coming, I'm coming!

00:29:28.924 --> 00:29:36.014
Can you all stand in front of the shop, please?

00:29:36.074 --> 00:29:37.886
Thank you, that's fine.

00:29:37.946 --> 00:29:40.497
Rather strange spelling on the shop sign.

00:29:40.557 --> 00:29:43.369
I thought the word Shakespeare had a piece.

00:29:43.389 --> 00:29:45.900
I can explain that, can't you, dear?

00:29:45.960 --> 00:29:47.051
Don't start again.

00:29:47.071 --> 00:29:49.452
I thought the topic was closed.

00:29:49.512 --> 00:29:50.632
Smile.

00:29:50.692 --> 00:29:53.150
And just one more picture.

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:54.877
Thank you very much.

00:29:54.938 --> 00:29:57.338
The article should be in the newspaper next week.

00:29:57.398 --> 00:29:58.789
Nice to meet you.

00:29:58.849 --> 00:30:00.110
Come again soon.

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:00.950
Goodbye Nancy.

00:30:01.010 --> 00:30:03.542
Goodbye Michael.

00:30:03.602 --> 00:30:06.042
Michael is it?

00:30:06.102 --> 00:30:07.733
Friend of yours is he?

00:30:07.793 --> 00:30:08.693
No.

00:30:08.754 --> 00:30:12.476
He's just a nice polite young man, that's all.

00:30:12.536 --> 00:30:15.547
Anyway, can't stand here chatting all day.

00:30:15.607 --> 00:30:16.497
Come on everyone.

00:30:16.557 --> 00:30:17.587
Back into the shop.

00:30:17.648 --> 00:30:21.234
There's work to do.

00:30:21.294 --> 00:30:25.671
I forgot the baking powder.

00:30:25.731 --> 00:30:28.073
I bought it yesterday, don't you remember?

00:30:28.133 --> 00:30:31.054
Yes, I know, but I forgot to tell Michael.

00:30:31.115 --> 00:30:32.166
Who?

00:30:32.226 --> 00:30:34.166
The journalist.

00:30:34.226 --> 00:30:36.268
He asked me for a recipe.

00:30:36.328 --> 00:30:39.029
I forgot to mention the baking powder.

00:30:39.089 --> 00:30:40.640
It's not important, is it?

00:30:40.700 --> 00:30:42.111
Of course it's important.

00:30:42.171 --> 00:30:45.213
Without the baking powder, there'll be as flat as a pancake.

00:30:45.273 --> 00:30:46.944
Don't worry so much.

00:30:47.004 --> 00:30:49.185
I think you should keep an eye on your mother.

00:30:49.246 --> 00:30:53.950
She's been at the sherry trifle and I think she's about to start singing.

00:30:54.010 --> 00:31:00.376
Oh, my darling, it's harder, Bob.

00:31:00.436 --> 00:31:06.710
Tourists from the USA.

00:31:06.770 --> 00:31:09.551
Come on, Sharon.

00:31:09.611 --> 00:31:11.182
You must see this.

00:31:11.242 --> 00:31:14.894
It's so quaint and so English.

00:31:14.954 --> 00:31:17.016
First, let's take some photos.

00:31:17.076 --> 00:31:18.938
It's lovely.

00:31:18.998 --> 00:31:20.158
Come on, Dave.

00:31:20.219 --> 00:31:23.670
I want to try some of those lovely little English cakes.

00:31:23.730 --> 00:31:26.161
I could eat an elephant right now.

00:31:26.221 --> 00:31:29.272
It's so cute in here.

00:31:29.332 --> 00:31:35.105
And look what the waitresses are wearing, just like in the movies.

00:31:35.165 --> 00:31:37.626
There's a table by the fireplace.

00:31:37.686 --> 00:31:38.848
Quick.

00:31:38.908 --> 00:31:40.480
Grand.

00:31:40.540 --> 00:31:41.771
Look.

00:31:41.832 --> 00:31:44.744
Two Americans next to the fireplace.

00:31:44.804 --> 00:31:45.355
Oh.

00:31:45.415 --> 00:31:48.358
She's so big she needs two chairs.

00:31:48.418 --> 00:31:51.342
And he's got a Simpsons t-shirt on.

00:31:51.402 --> 00:31:53.434
This is going to be fun.

00:31:53.494 --> 00:31:54.785
Just watch me, Grand.

00:31:54.845 --> 00:31:56.376
What are you going to do?

00:31:56.396 --> 00:31:58.398
You watch.

00:31:58.458 --> 00:32:03.332
Welcome to our famous Shakespearean t-shop.

00:32:03.392 --> 00:32:07.465
It is indeed an honor to welcome you to our fine establishment.

00:32:07.525 --> 00:32:09.946
What sustenance can I provide?

00:32:10.007 --> 00:32:11.808
How did you say?

00:32:11.868 --> 00:32:13.710
Are you talking English?

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:16.872
Ah, of course he is, dear.

00:32:16.933 --> 00:32:19.674
Don't take any notice of him.

00:32:19.734 --> 00:32:22.436
I just love your quaint accent.

00:32:22.496 --> 00:32:24.968
It gives me goose bibbles.

00:32:25.029 --> 00:32:27.440
We want to try everything.

00:32:27.500 --> 00:32:29.102
Bring us a piece of...

00:32:29.163 --> 00:32:30.314
Everything.

00:32:30.374 --> 00:32:32.826
Oh, you are so terrible, Tom.

00:32:32.846 --> 00:32:34.497
That's too much.

00:32:34.557 --> 00:32:40.492
We want to both start with your Caesar cream tea with lots of extra cream.

00:32:40.552 --> 00:32:44.174
Why not try the William Woolnut Buns?

00:32:44.234 --> 00:32:49.268
A secret recipe was handed down through our family, the Shakespeare's.

00:32:49.328 --> 00:32:52.491
Sure thing, four of those is way.

00:32:52.551 --> 00:32:58.025
What do you mean, secret recipe was handed down through our family?

00:32:58.085 --> 00:33:02.018
Do you mean that you're related to Willy Shakespeare?

00:33:02.078 --> 00:33:03.519
Yes!

00:33:03.580 --> 00:33:06.182
But don't tell everyone, please.

00:33:06.242 --> 00:33:08.565
We don't like to tell too many people.

00:33:08.625 --> 00:33:11.317
Oh, you English are so reserved.

00:33:11.378 --> 00:33:14.510
I promise we won't tell anyone.

00:33:14.570 --> 00:33:15.992
Alright then.

00:33:16.052 --> 00:33:17.323
I can tell you.

00:33:17.383 --> 00:33:18.154
What?

00:33:18.214 --> 00:33:19.812
What?

00:33:19.872 --> 00:33:24.786
William Shakespeare's my father's great-grandfather.

00:33:24.846 --> 00:33:26.928
Did you hear that, Sharon?

00:33:26.988 --> 00:33:28.190
Her father is...

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:30.863
Not so well, Tom Deere.

00:33:30.923 --> 00:33:33.495
That's so awesome.

00:33:33.555 --> 00:33:34.545
Yes!

00:33:34.605 --> 00:33:37.566
And he's here in the shop at the moment.

00:33:37.626 --> 00:33:38.757
Wow!

00:33:38.817 --> 00:33:41.728
Well, over there behind the counter.

00:33:41.788 --> 00:33:43.058
Oh, yeah.

00:33:43.118 --> 00:33:46.930
He even looks like Willy Shakespeare, don't you think, babe?

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:50.041
I must have a photo for the folks back home.

00:33:50.101 --> 00:33:52.002
We must introduce ourselves.

00:33:52.062 --> 00:33:53.162
Come on, babe.

00:33:53.222 --> 00:33:55.413
Oh, don't tell him I said anything.

00:33:55.473 --> 00:33:56.493
Promise?

00:33:56.554 --> 00:33:58.464
Mom's the word.

00:33:58.524 --> 00:34:00.864
And one other thing.

00:34:00.924 --> 00:34:04.834
He is a close personal friend of the Queen.

00:34:04.894 --> 00:34:07.617
So please bow when you speak to him.

00:34:07.677 --> 00:34:10.279
This is so exciting.

00:34:10.339 --> 00:34:14.242
People back home simply won't believe us.

00:34:14.302 --> 00:34:18.335
Good morning, your royal highness.

00:34:18.396 --> 00:34:19.776
Love all.

00:34:19.837 --> 00:34:21.168
Trust few.

00:34:21.228 --> 00:34:23.440
Do wrong to none.

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:25.500
Great lines.

00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:26.872
Sorry.

00:34:26.932 --> 00:34:28.223
Sorry, sir.

00:34:28.283 --> 00:34:29.862
Shakes be.

00:34:29.922 --> 00:34:38.364
We're Sharon and Tom Jordan from Houston, Texas, in the wonderful United States of America.

00:34:38.423 --> 00:34:39.984
Are you all right?

00:34:40.044 --> 00:34:43.326
We certainly are your...rileness.

00:34:43.386 --> 00:34:44.296
Why?

00:34:44.356 --> 00:34:45.706
What?

00:34:45.767 --> 00:34:48.348
Do you both have a background?

00:34:48.408 --> 00:34:50.438
No, so willy.

00:34:50.499 --> 00:34:55.901
I wonder if I could have a photo of you standing with my Tom.

00:34:55.961 --> 00:34:59.022
For the people back home, you understand?

00:34:59.082 --> 00:35:00.466
Yes, but...

00:35:00.526 --> 00:35:03.029
Why are you bending over all the time?

00:35:03.089 --> 00:35:04.670
Go on, Tom, dear.

00:35:04.730 --> 00:35:06.501
Stand next to Sir Willy.

00:35:06.561 --> 00:35:07.802
Yes, dear.

00:35:07.862 --> 00:35:09.944
Love the clothes, Sir Willy.

00:35:10.004 --> 00:35:12.305
Ah, yes, I can explain that.

00:35:12.365 --> 00:35:14.306
They are not my idea.

00:35:14.366 --> 00:35:21.510
Tell me, Sir Willy, apt, when you're next at the palace, we'd love an invitation to meet...

00:35:21.571 --> 00:35:23.101
You know who?

00:35:23.162 --> 00:35:25.763
Perhaps you could mention our names.

00:35:25.823 --> 00:35:28.875
Say that we like riding horses, too.

00:35:28.935 --> 00:35:32.136
We also eat their health food jam.

00:35:32.196 --> 00:35:34.177
We get them in the States now.

00:35:34.237 --> 00:35:38.578
We love the raspberry jam, but not crazy about the gooseberry.

00:35:38.638 --> 00:35:41.119
The seeds get under my teeth.

00:35:41.159 --> 00:35:44.711
Sorry, do you need a doctor or maybe a psychiatrist?

00:35:44.771 --> 00:35:46.231
There's a good one in the village.

00:35:46.291 --> 00:35:49.230
Smile, say cheese!

00:35:49.290 --> 00:35:51.460
Thank you so willy.

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:59.304
My pleasure, but by the way, yes, we love your plays and especially the movie.

00:35:59.364 --> 00:36:00.926
What's it called babe?

00:36:00.986 --> 00:36:03.330
It's on the tip of my tongue.

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:06.386
Shakespeare is not asking about our marriage.

00:36:06.446 --> 00:36:09.768
He gave you the title of the film.

00:36:09.828 --> 00:36:23.230
Shakespeare in love?

00:36:23.290 --> 00:36:38.173
Tell me, sir, why didn't you get Johnny Depp to do it?

00:36:38.233 --> 00:36:39.384
Much better actor.

00:36:39.444 --> 00:36:40.966
What are you talking?

00:36:40.986 --> 00:36:42.116
Come on, dear.

00:36:42.176 --> 00:36:44.619
Thank you, Sir Shakespeare.

00:36:44.679 --> 00:36:46.040
Yes, you're right, dear.

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:48.052
Thanks a bunch, Sir Willy.

00:36:48.112 --> 00:36:51.884
Going to try one or two of your secret willy walnut bones.

00:36:51.944 --> 00:36:53.716
Bye.

00:36:53.776 --> 00:36:56.218
Bloody Americans.

00:36:56.279 --> 00:36:59.311
Why didn't Christopher Columbus stay at home by the fire?

00:36:59.371 --> 00:37:05.059
Then he wouldn't have discovered America.

00:37:05.119 --> 00:37:10.506
Did you enjoy your little chat?

00:37:10.566 --> 00:37:14.387
Very much, but I think he was a little confused.

00:37:14.447 --> 00:37:16.237
Excuse me, dear.

00:37:16.298 --> 00:37:19.558
Can you tell me where the rest room is?

00:37:19.618 --> 00:37:23.240
If you mean the toilets, they're outside at the bottom of the garden.

00:37:23.300 --> 00:37:24.170
What?

00:37:24.230 --> 00:37:25.832
They're in the garden?

00:37:25.892 --> 00:37:27.183
I have to see these.

00:37:27.243 --> 00:37:30.975
Yes, and they're the same as in William Shakespeare's day.

00:37:31.035 --> 00:37:35.448
Do you mean that Willie Shakespeare actually sat on your toilets?

00:37:35.508 --> 00:37:36.469
Oh, yes.

00:37:36.529 --> 00:37:39.641
He lived here as a child before he went to Stratford.

00:37:39.701 --> 00:37:40.752
Come on, babe.

00:37:40.812 --> 00:37:43.133
We must sit on the toilet.

00:37:43.193 --> 00:37:45.024
Feel where Willie sat.

00:37:45.084 --> 00:37:46.796
I'm coming with you, dear.

00:37:46.856 --> 00:37:51.411
I can't miss this.

00:37:51.472 --> 00:37:56.326
I heard that, Josie.

00:37:56.386 --> 00:37:58.847
You are so naughty.

00:37:58.907 --> 00:38:02.548
Well, Americans can be so stupid.

00:38:02.568 --> 00:38:04.278
They believe anything.

00:38:04.338 --> 00:38:07.489
What is the matter with those two Americans?

00:38:07.549 --> 00:38:09.729
I think they both have a screw loose.

00:38:09.789 --> 00:38:12.520
I have no idea what they were talking about.

00:38:12.580 --> 00:38:15.531
Yes, they are a little strange.

00:38:15.591 --> 00:38:17.331
I don't know why, Dad.

00:38:17.391 --> 00:38:18.622
No idea.

00:38:18.682 --> 00:38:23.606
No idea at all.

00:38:23.667 --> 00:38:32.534
Josie's Revenge.

00:38:32.594 --> 00:38:35.046
Just look at this, everyone.

00:38:35.106 --> 00:38:36.327
It's wonderful.

00:38:36.347 --> 00:38:38.869
Ooh, we're on the front page.

00:38:38.929 --> 00:38:40.670
Look, Josie.

00:38:40.730 --> 00:38:42.191
Nice.

00:38:42.251 --> 00:38:43.472
Hmm.

00:38:43.532 --> 00:38:45.093
Yes, you're right.

00:38:45.153 --> 00:38:47.845
It's really good publicity for the tea shop.

00:38:47.905 --> 00:38:51.138
I hope we get more people coming in than the last few days.

00:38:51.198 --> 00:38:53.561
Don't worry so much, dear.

00:38:53.621 --> 00:38:54.822
I do worry.

00:38:54.842 --> 00:38:56.472
I worry a lot.

00:38:56.532 --> 00:38:58.043
I don't think that...

00:38:58.063 --> 00:39:04.686
It says here that the tea and cakes are delicious and the atmosphere is truly old-fashioned.

00:39:04.746 --> 00:39:14.271
And then he finishes the article with some comment that the owners are a bunch of real individuals who reflect the eccentricities of English village life.

00:39:14.331 --> 00:39:16.552
What exactly does it mean?

00:39:16.612 --> 00:39:18.422
It's an insult.

00:39:18.482 --> 00:39:20.843
He could have taken a better photo.

00:39:20.903 --> 00:39:22.133
I like it.

00:39:22.193 --> 00:39:24.884
Yes, well, you look lovely in it.

00:39:24.944 --> 00:39:29.016
Why are you holding a plate of doughnuts in front of you like that?

00:39:29.076 --> 00:39:30.696
Why do you think?

00:39:30.717 --> 00:39:35.878
Yes, but it does look a bit peculiar with a plate and two doughnuts down there.

00:39:35.899 --> 00:39:37.579
It looks as if you...

00:39:37.599 --> 00:39:38.790
Yes, thank you.

00:39:38.850 --> 00:39:40.760
Come on, Nancy, back to work.

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:43.672
The shop opens in 30 minutes.

00:39:43.732 --> 00:39:47.690
Gran, yes, Josephine.

00:39:47.750 --> 00:39:52.333
Why is everyone talking about this bloody, sodding tea shop?

00:39:52.394 --> 00:39:53.755
Yes, I'm no love.

00:39:53.816 --> 00:39:57.080
But the only bleeding topic is recipes for cakes.

00:39:57.140 --> 00:40:00.014
Shall we have dark peeling or old grey?

00:40:00.074 --> 00:40:04.058
I think it's dark geelling and oh grey, Josie.

00:40:04.118 --> 00:40:06.402
Or who came into the shop?

00:40:06.462 --> 00:40:10.803
Do you know that Mrs. Notface came in yesterday and she had two?

00:40:10.863 --> 00:40:14.584
Yes, two slices of big, well-taught.

00:40:14.645 --> 00:40:17.985
Very good, Josie, very good.

00:40:18.005 --> 00:40:20.396
I'm not trying to be funny, Grand.

00:40:20.456 --> 00:40:24.478
And mum and dad seem to think I should always help in the shop.

00:40:24.538 --> 00:40:27.458
No, I don't have any free time to meet my friends.

00:40:27.518 --> 00:40:33.910
And every time I ask mum about how much she's going to pay me, she says, we can talk about it later.

00:40:33.930 --> 00:40:35.888
Shall I speak to your mother?

00:40:35.949 --> 00:40:37.316
Thanks, Grand.

00:40:37.376 --> 00:40:39.497
That would be great.

00:40:39.557 --> 00:40:41.118
Grand?

00:40:41.178 --> 00:40:43.168
Yes, Josie.

00:40:43.228 --> 00:40:44.839
Can you help me?

00:40:44.899 --> 00:40:46.339
Of course.

00:40:46.399 --> 00:40:48.239
What can I do?

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:52.811
Well, I want to play a joke on mum and dad.

00:40:52.871 --> 00:40:55.162
I like jokes.

00:40:55.222 --> 00:41:01.984
What do you mean, put salt in the sugar bowl or unscrew the door handles?

00:41:02.044 --> 00:41:03.491
No, Grand.

00:41:03.551 --> 00:41:06.652
Tie their shoelaces together.

00:41:06.712 --> 00:41:07.982
Grand!

00:41:08.042 --> 00:41:09.966
Something much more subtle.

00:41:10.026 --> 00:41:12.188
What do you want to do?

00:41:12.248 --> 00:41:14.821
Well, uh...

00:41:14.881 --> 00:41:19.374
I could phone up and pretend to be a health inspector.

00:41:19.434 --> 00:41:21.294
Why would you do that?

00:41:21.354 --> 00:41:23.195
None of us are ill.

00:41:23.255 --> 00:41:25.226
My back aches a bit.

00:41:25.286 --> 00:41:27.527
I have some problems in the night.

00:41:27.587 --> 00:41:29.968
No, Grand, you don't understand.

00:41:29.988 --> 00:41:33.509
A health inspector for the tea shop, not for you.

00:41:33.529 --> 00:41:36.004
Oh, I see.

00:41:36.064 --> 00:41:38.280
No, I don't.

00:41:38.300 --> 00:41:43.434
Erm, a health inspector comes and checks the kitchens to see if they are clean.

00:41:43.494 --> 00:41:46.737
But the kitchen is sparkless, I clean it myself.

00:41:46.797 --> 00:41:48.398
Yes, I know it is.

00:41:48.458 --> 00:41:53.742
But Mum and Dad will be frantic with worry and they get so stressed out.

00:41:53.802 --> 00:41:54.934
It'll be fun.

00:41:54.954 --> 00:41:56.936
I don't know.

00:41:56.996 --> 00:41:59.561
Well, I want to try.

00:41:59.621 --> 00:42:02.666
What name should I use when I phone up?

00:42:02.726 --> 00:42:05.138
Are you really sure about this, Josie?

00:42:05.198 --> 00:42:06.289
Yes, I am.

00:42:06.309 --> 00:42:08.961
I think a man's voice is better.

00:42:09.021 --> 00:42:10.592
Let me try.

00:42:10.652 --> 00:42:12.914
Good afternoon, madam.

00:42:12.975 --> 00:42:14.956
My name is Mr. Chumbly.

00:42:14.976 --> 00:42:19.079
Oh, that's very good, Josie.

00:42:19.139 --> 00:42:20.940
Come on, let me try.

00:42:20.960 --> 00:42:30.745
I'll go outside and use my mobile.

00:42:30.805 --> 00:42:43.271
So am I, but that doesn't seem to be important.

00:42:43.291 --> 00:42:51.126
And if it's Mr Chatterly wanting to come and discuss having afternoon tea for his society members, tell him any time is alright.

00:42:51.186 --> 00:42:52.947
Yes dear.

00:42:53.008 --> 00:42:56.559
The old Shakespeare tea shop, how can I help you?

00:42:56.619 --> 00:42:58.600
Good afternoon, sir.

00:42:58.660 --> 00:43:02.192
Mr. Chumley, health inspector service speaking.

00:43:02.252 --> 00:43:03.284
Ah, yes.

00:43:03.344 --> 00:43:05.416
My wife said you, my phone.

00:43:05.476 --> 00:43:06.516
Yes.

00:43:06.576 --> 00:43:10.880
It's just an informal visit to have a look around in the next few days.

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:12.602
No need to worry, sir.

00:43:12.662 --> 00:43:15.895
My wife said that any time this week is fine.

00:43:15.955 --> 00:43:16.626
Thank you.

00:43:16.686 --> 00:43:17.217
Goodbye.

00:43:17.237 --> 00:43:20.363
Goodbye, sir.

00:43:20.423 --> 00:43:24.399
You were marvellous, Josie.

00:43:24.459 --> 00:43:26.110
Yes, I was good, wasn't I?

00:43:26.170 --> 00:43:28.271
Do you think they believed you?

00:43:28.291 --> 00:43:29.302
I don't know.

00:43:29.362 --> 00:43:33.434
Dad answered the phone, so I'm sure he'll get the message wrong when he tells Mum.

00:43:33.494 --> 00:43:34.985
And then they will argue.

00:43:35.045 --> 00:43:41.579
Oh, you are naughty.

00:43:41.639 --> 00:43:44.671
That was a bit strange.

00:43:44.731 --> 00:43:45.962
What's the matter?

00:43:46.022 --> 00:43:49.983
He had a peculiar voice, sounded familiar.

00:43:50.043 --> 00:43:52.213
Was it Mr Chatterly on the phone?

00:43:52.273 --> 00:43:53.363
I think so.

00:43:53.423 --> 00:43:54.314
Yes?

00:43:54.374 --> 00:43:57.906
Is Mr Chatterly from the Health Inspector service?

00:43:57.966 --> 00:43:59.306
Sorry.

00:43:59.366 --> 00:44:00.437
Hi.

00:44:00.497 --> 00:44:03.588
He said he was coming to check everything is okay.

00:44:03.648 --> 00:44:04.878
What?

00:44:04.938 --> 00:44:06.539
Health Inspector?

00:44:06.599 --> 00:44:07.760
Here.

00:44:07.820 --> 00:44:11.452
Mr Chatterly is Chairman of the Marrow and Cucumber Society.

00:44:11.472 --> 00:44:13.442
He's not a Health Inspector.

00:44:13.502 --> 00:44:14.293
But...

00:44:14.313 --> 00:44:15.473
but Pete!

00:44:15.534 --> 00:44:19.384
If you had a brain you would be dangerous.

00:44:19.444 --> 00:44:22.183
Is there anything between your ears?

00:44:22.243 --> 00:44:23.644
I don't understand.

00:44:23.704 --> 00:44:25.725
Why didn't I answer the phone?

00:44:25.785 --> 00:44:28.727
Why didn't you let me answer the phone?

00:44:28.787 --> 00:44:29.877
Sometimes.

00:44:29.917 --> 00:44:32.018
But you told me.

00:44:32.078 --> 00:44:36.031
It doesn't matter, dear.

00:44:36.091 --> 00:44:42.154
Problems.

00:44:42.214 --> 00:44:46.832
What are you doing, dear?

00:44:46.893 --> 00:44:49.409
What are all these bills on the table?

00:44:49.469 --> 00:44:50.590
Look at this.

00:44:50.650 --> 00:44:51.920
And this.

00:44:51.980 --> 00:44:53.231
How much?

00:44:53.291 --> 00:44:55.042
I can't believe it.

00:44:55.102 --> 00:44:56.752
Look at this one.

00:44:56.812 --> 00:45:02.145
The insurance for the shop costs more than the value of a Manchester United football club.

00:45:02.206 --> 00:45:04.227
Don't exaggerate Pete.

00:45:04.287 --> 00:45:07.058
There just aren't enough customers coming into the shop.

00:45:07.118 --> 00:45:08.869
We can't pay the bills.

00:45:08.929 --> 00:45:12.611
I think it was a bad idea to open a tee shop, Nancy.

00:45:12.671 --> 00:45:16.516
I think we have to close the shop down before it's too late.

00:45:16.576 --> 00:45:19.232
There must be an alternative.

00:45:19.292 --> 00:45:20.224
There isn't.

00:45:20.284 --> 00:45:24.178
Yes, but we just have to work harder.

00:45:24.238 --> 00:45:26.781
It's not a question of working harder.

00:45:26.841 --> 00:45:28.804
We don't make enough money.

00:45:28.864 --> 00:45:31.286
Josie can't help any more than she does.

00:45:31.346 --> 00:45:33.439
She does have to go to school, Nancy.

00:45:33.499 --> 00:45:34.984
I know, I know.

00:45:35.044 --> 00:45:38.110
and we can't afford to have staff to support us.

00:45:38.170 --> 00:45:42.644
You and I are working all day and sometimes it seems most of the night as well.

00:45:42.704 --> 00:45:49.310
And your mother does her best, but she's getting on in years and you can't always rely on her.

00:45:49.370 --> 00:45:50.683
What do you mean?

00:45:50.743 --> 00:45:53.324
Well, look what happened yesterday.

00:45:53.384 --> 00:45:58.695
She got in a muddle with the orders and one couple didn't pay and another paid double.

00:45:58.755 --> 00:46:01.165
It was all sorted out eventually.

00:46:01.225 --> 00:46:02.175
Yes.

00:46:02.235 --> 00:46:09.046
Only after one couple phoned their solicitor and I had to follow the Stratford bus in my car to catch the other couple.

00:46:09.106 --> 00:46:12.497
Do you know how many stops there are between here and Stratford?

00:46:12.557 --> 00:46:14.027
Do you know how many?

00:46:14.087 --> 00:46:15.517
No, I don't.

00:46:15.577 --> 00:46:15.998
27!

00:46:16.058 --> 00:46:17.968
27!

00:46:18.028 --> 00:46:22.789
And when I found them, it turned out that they had paid your mother.

00:46:22.849 --> 00:46:25.509
She had put the money in her pockets and forgotten about it.

00:46:25.529 --> 00:46:29.250
I mean, she drives me crazy.

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:31.580
What do you suggest we do?

00:46:31.640 --> 00:46:36.091
Well, we could set fire to the shop and claim on the insurance.

00:46:36.151 --> 00:46:38.362
You're not serious, are you?

00:46:38.422 --> 00:46:39.582
What do you think?

00:46:39.642 --> 00:46:42.342
Of course I'm not serious.

00:46:42.402 --> 00:46:46.284
But it's an idea, then.

00:46:46.344 --> 00:46:49.098
Oh.

00:46:49.158 --> 00:46:52.594
Hello Mum and Josie.

00:46:52.654 --> 00:46:54.184
Can you both come here?

00:46:54.224 --> 00:46:57.684
We need to talk.

00:46:57.745 --> 00:47:02.556
I think we have to close down the shop.

00:47:02.616 --> 00:47:03.896
Oh no!

00:47:03.956 --> 00:47:04.846
Why?

00:47:04.866 --> 00:47:07.086
It's such fun.

00:47:07.106 --> 00:47:10.777
I think I'm going to cry.

00:47:10.837 --> 00:47:14.358
Please don't probably strangle you if you do.

00:47:14.418 --> 00:47:17.392
Just listen to Pete everyone.

00:47:17.452 --> 00:47:21.308
The simple fact is that we are losing money fast.

00:47:21.368 --> 00:47:25.111
There just aren't enough tourists coming in and the locals don't spend enough.

00:47:25.171 --> 00:47:26.862
Yes, you're right.

00:47:26.922 --> 00:47:33.587
Most tables are filled by old ladies who sit there all morning with one cup of tea and a fruit bun.

00:47:33.647 --> 00:47:38.910
Sometimes I see them getting their own biscuits and cakes out of their shopping bags.

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:43.674
Perhaps we should body search all the customers as they come in the shop.

00:47:43.734 --> 00:47:47.888
Sometimes I wonder what goes on inside your mother's head, Nancy.

00:47:47.948 --> 00:47:50.670
Have you got any ideas, Josie?

00:47:50.730 --> 00:47:54.251
You're the intelligent one in the family.

00:47:54.311 --> 00:47:57.682
We need some promotions.

00:47:57.742 --> 00:47:59.583
I want to be promoted.

00:47:59.603 --> 00:48:02.304
I want to be the manager-ish.

00:48:02.324 --> 00:48:03.314
No, Fran.

00:48:03.374 --> 00:48:05.645
I don't mean that type of promotion.

00:48:05.705 --> 00:48:11.077
I mean, we need some new ideas to get people interested in coming into the shop.

00:48:11.137 --> 00:48:12.577
Oh, I see.

00:48:12.637 --> 00:48:14.410
Silly me.

00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:27.183
Why don't I have a table outside the tea shop and I can sell some Shakespeare souvenirs and read out some famous Shakespeare sayings to get people to stop?

00:48:27.243 --> 00:48:29.365
That's a nice idea, Mum.

00:48:29.425 --> 00:48:31.787
I know what you can do, Dad.

00:48:31.847 --> 00:48:35.150
Am I going to like this idea or not?

00:48:35.210 --> 00:48:39.754
Well, you said that we aren't getting enough tourists in the shop.

00:48:39.814 --> 00:48:41.225
Yes.

00:48:41.285 --> 00:48:43.468
Where are all the tourists?

00:48:43.528 --> 00:48:45.739
in Stratford perhaps?

00:48:45.799 --> 00:48:47.190
That's right.

00:48:47.250 --> 00:48:52.482
You and Boris can walk through Stratford giving out leaflets to all the tourists.

00:48:52.542 --> 00:48:55.713
Boris can wear something as well to look medieval.

00:48:55.773 --> 00:48:58.754
Only if your mother's clothes would be perfect Nancy.

00:48:58.814 --> 00:49:08.337
You can have a 10% voucher on the leaflet dad and you can carry a sandwich board with a big poster advertising the t-shop on it.

00:49:08.397 --> 00:49:11.447
You look great in your Shakespeare costume.

00:49:11.507 --> 00:49:17.131
You can read out bits of Shakespeare and have Elizabethan music playing in the background.

00:49:17.191 --> 00:49:20.163
I knew I wasn't going to like the idea.

00:49:20.224 --> 00:49:24.827
But desperate situations call for desperate measures.

00:49:24.887 --> 00:49:29.771
If we don't get more customers in the shop in the next couple of weeks then we have to close.

00:49:29.831 --> 00:49:31.598
That's final.

00:49:31.658 --> 00:49:33.584
Come on then everyone!

00:49:33.644 --> 00:49:35.384
Let's get started!

00:49:35.444 --> 00:49:38.086
No time to lose!

00:49:38.146 --> 00:49:47.572
What?

00:49:47.632 --> 00:49:57.798
What are you doing, Gran?

00:49:57.858 --> 00:50:03.706
I want to put some balloons on the table, make people stop and look.

00:50:03.766 --> 00:50:06.499
What do you think?

00:50:06.559 --> 00:50:09.713
It's a cool idea with the table in front of the shop.

00:50:09.773 --> 00:50:12.005
Oh, thank you.

00:50:12.065 --> 00:50:15.406
Ooh, this is not so easy!

00:50:15.466 --> 00:50:17.267
Oops!

00:50:17.327 --> 00:50:20.378
I thought you wanted to sell Shakespeare souvenirs.

00:50:20.438 --> 00:50:25.359
I'm not sure that your pink hand knitted bed socks are very Shakespearean.

00:50:25.419 --> 00:50:32.050
I'm going to tell people the knitting pattern is...

00:50:32.110 --> 00:50:34.241
from Shakespeare's mother.

00:50:34.301 --> 00:50:36.261
So easy with the balloons, Gran.

00:50:36.321 --> 00:50:38.132
Don't overdo it.

00:50:38.192 --> 00:50:42.773
Ooh, this is fun.

00:50:42.833 --> 00:50:47.174
Oh dear.

00:50:47.234 --> 00:50:49.665
Oh dear.

00:50:49.725 --> 00:50:52.156
I told you to be careful.

00:50:52.216 --> 00:50:54.897
Oh, I don't feel...

00:50:54.957 --> 00:50:56.777
Oh, go on, go on!

00:50:56.838 --> 00:50:58.238
Oh no, what's the matter?

00:50:58.298 --> 00:50:58.748
Help!

00:50:58.808 --> 00:50:59.588
Someone!

00:50:59.648 --> 00:51:00.309
Help!

00:51:00.369 --> 00:51:01.772
Please!

00:51:01.832 --> 00:51:03.036
Mom!

00:51:03.096 --> 00:51:03.657
Dad!

00:51:03.717 --> 00:51:04.527
Help!

00:51:04.588 --> 00:51:05.808
What's the matter, Josie?

00:51:05.868 --> 00:51:06.899
It's Grand!

00:51:06.959 --> 00:51:08.440
She was blowing up balloons.

00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:10.481
I told her to stop, but she didn't.

00:51:10.541 --> 00:51:11.562
She's collapsed.

00:51:11.622 --> 00:51:13.033
I think she's dead.

00:51:13.073 --> 00:51:16.476
She's just painted, silly old fool.

00:51:16.536 --> 00:51:17.826
Leave it to me.

00:51:17.886 --> 00:51:19.397
What are you doing, Dad?

00:51:19.457 --> 00:51:22.449
Leave it to me, you see.

00:51:22.509 --> 00:51:25.481
No, Dad, don't do that.

00:51:25.501 --> 00:51:27.176
Oh!

00:51:27.236 --> 00:51:29.761
This always works.

00:51:29.821 --> 00:51:31.242
Come on, wake up!

00:51:31.302 --> 00:51:33.912
What's happening here?

00:51:33.972 --> 00:51:35.702
Mum!

00:51:35.762 --> 00:51:37.453
What are you doing, Pete?

00:51:37.513 --> 00:51:38.993
The old fool fainted.

00:51:39.033 --> 00:51:41.224
I just threw a jug of water over her.

00:51:41.284 --> 00:51:42.644
You idiot!

00:51:42.704 --> 00:51:44.114
Phone for an ambulance!

00:51:44.174 --> 00:51:46.055
But don't as I tell you!

00:51:46.115 --> 00:51:47.025
Now!

00:51:47.085 --> 00:51:47.845
Alright.

00:51:47.905 --> 00:51:49.506
Alright, I'm going.

00:51:49.566 --> 00:51:55.736
What a week.

00:51:55.796 --> 00:52:03.278
Oh, I feel a bit better now.

00:52:03.339 --> 00:52:05.471
Oh, what happened?

00:52:05.532 --> 00:52:07.213
I feel wet.

00:52:07.273 --> 00:52:09.306
You're all right now, Mum.

00:52:09.366 --> 00:52:11.097
The ambulance is here now.

00:52:11.157 --> 00:52:13.570
Everything's going to be all right.

00:52:13.630 --> 00:52:15.072
Come on, Boris.

00:52:15.132 --> 00:52:16.703
Let's drive to Stratford.

00:52:16.764 --> 00:52:32.406
I think it's better if we keep away from Nancy for a few hours, don't you?

00:52:32.466 --> 00:52:45.124
Here we are, Boris.

00:52:45.184 --> 00:52:48.085
I hope we don't meet anyone we know in Stratford.

00:52:48.145 --> 00:52:52.056
We both look completely ridiculous.

00:52:52.116 --> 00:52:55.346
Love the tights, mate.

00:52:55.406 --> 00:52:57.836
I knew this wasn't a good idea.

00:52:57.896 --> 00:52:59.847
Come on, let's start.

00:52:59.907 --> 00:53:02.877
We can put some leaflets under the windscreen wipers.

00:53:02.937 --> 00:53:05.858
Good idea, eh?

00:53:05.918 --> 00:53:08.908
This sandwich board is so awkward and heavy.

00:53:08.968 --> 00:53:11.529
What a stupid idea, eh?

00:53:11.589 --> 00:53:13.773
What's the matter, Boris?

00:53:13.833 --> 00:53:16.388
Oh, it's a cat.

00:53:16.448 --> 00:53:17.588
No, what?

00:53:17.648 --> 00:53:18.368
Stop it.

00:53:18.428 --> 00:53:20.628
Oh, come back.

00:53:20.688 --> 00:53:22.789
Oh, the lead's caught around my legs.

00:53:22.829 --> 00:53:25.679
Oh, oh, oh.

00:53:25.740 --> 00:53:27.410
Oh, my God.

00:53:27.430 --> 00:53:28.880
I'll be MW.

00:53:28.940 --> 00:53:32.211
Whoops.

00:53:32.271 --> 00:53:34.562
That was a wing mirror.

00:53:34.622 --> 00:53:35.532
Stop it, Boris.

00:53:35.592 --> 00:53:36.842
Calm down.

00:53:36.902 --> 00:53:38.392
Look what you've done.

00:53:38.453 --> 00:53:39.203
Come on.

00:53:39.263 --> 00:53:43.149
Before anyone sees us.

00:53:43.209 --> 00:53:49.416
We can stand here outside Marks and Spencers for us.

00:53:49.476 --> 00:53:52.857
Just sit quietly and look sweet and cuddly.

00:53:52.917 --> 00:53:54.378
Please.

00:53:54.438 --> 00:53:58.738
Okay, here goes.

00:53:58.799 --> 00:54:04.470
The only Shakespeare tea shopping in Wire Piddle.

00:54:04.530 --> 00:54:10.372
This gets uncaked, freshly baked, using Shakespeare's own recipes.

00:54:10.432 --> 00:54:11.933
Hello, madam, please.

00:54:11.973 --> 00:54:12.783
Have a leaflet.

00:54:12.843 --> 00:54:14.294
Thank you very much.

00:54:14.354 --> 00:54:17.236
Visit Shakespeare's favorite tee shop.

00:54:17.296 --> 00:54:19.687
To be or not to be.

00:54:19.747 --> 00:54:21.738
That is the question.

00:54:21.798 --> 00:54:23.119
Thank you, sir.

00:54:23.179 --> 00:54:24.160
Madam.

00:54:24.220 --> 00:54:25.761
I like your dog.

00:54:25.821 --> 00:54:26.771
Yes, madam.

00:54:26.831 --> 00:54:27.592
Thank you.

00:54:27.652 --> 00:54:29.923
Yee-o-dee Shakespeare.

00:54:29.983 --> 00:54:31.184
What is name?

00:54:31.244 --> 00:54:31.914
Sorry?

00:54:31.975 --> 00:54:32.675
What?

00:54:32.735 --> 00:54:34.306
It's Boris.

00:54:34.366 --> 00:54:36.487
Yee-o-dee Shakespeare.

00:54:36.507 --> 00:54:37.641
That's an old name.

00:54:37.702 --> 00:54:38.775
You should know.

00:54:38.835 --> 00:54:39.926
I heard that.

00:54:39.986 --> 00:54:42.496
Could you just go away, please, madam?

00:54:42.556 --> 00:54:46.418
I'm trying to advertise our world-famous T-Shop.

00:54:46.478 --> 00:54:50.749
You owe the Shakespeare T-Shop-E in wire piddle.

00:54:50.809 --> 00:54:53.390
Visit Shakespeare's favorite T-Shop.

00:54:53.450 --> 00:54:56.700
Visit the house where he lived as a child.

00:54:56.760 --> 00:54:59.541
William Shakespeare never lived in wire piddle.

00:54:59.601 --> 00:55:01.262
Yes, he did.

00:55:01.322 --> 00:55:04.662
Before he went to war.

00:55:04.722 --> 00:55:08.525
Before he went to war, no he didn't.

00:55:08.586 --> 00:55:10.106
What are you talking about?

00:55:10.167 --> 00:55:14.760
Before he was called to the front.

00:55:14.820 --> 00:55:16.492
He never went to war?

00:55:16.552 --> 00:55:17.512
Yes he did.

00:55:17.572 --> 00:55:19.194
It's before your time.

00:55:19.254 --> 00:55:20.865
But only just.

00:55:20.925 --> 00:55:22.897
You are talking complete rubbish.

00:55:22.957 --> 00:55:24.581
Just go away madam.

00:55:24.641 --> 00:55:32.428
The oldies Shakespeare tea-shopping in wire-piddle, traditional English tea shop with traditional homemade cakes.

00:55:32.489 --> 00:55:34.199
Here's a pamphlet for you, madam.

00:55:34.260 --> 00:55:35.440
Thank you.

00:55:35.500 --> 00:55:44.626
As William Shakespeare wrote in Macbeth, I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears, and sweat.

00:55:44.686 --> 00:55:48.540
William Shakespeare didn't write that, but Winston Churchill wrote it.

00:55:48.600 --> 00:55:49.441
No, he didn't.

00:55:49.501 --> 00:55:50.161
Yes, he did.

00:55:50.181 --> 00:55:51.322
I should know.

00:55:51.382 --> 00:55:55.502
And why are you suddenly an expert on Shakespeare?

00:55:55.562 --> 00:55:58.893
I'm an historian from the local museum.

00:55:58.953 --> 00:56:00.964
Ah, yes.

00:56:01.024 --> 00:56:02.194
Well, ah-ha.

00:56:02.254 --> 00:56:11.796
That William Shakespeare didn't actually write it, but Winston Churchill borrowed the idea from him.

00:56:11.856 --> 00:56:14.907
Anyway, I can't stand here talking to you all day.

00:56:14.967 --> 00:56:17.130
Come on, Boris.

00:56:17.190 --> 00:56:22.056
Some people have no idea who they Boris.

00:56:22.116 --> 00:56:24.738
It's the Churchill.

00:56:24.798 --> 00:56:26.920
No idea.

00:56:26.980 --> 00:56:29.922
Only another 900 Leaklets to give out.

00:56:29.982 --> 00:56:30.702
Come on.

00:56:30.762 --> 00:56:34.760
Let's get on with this and then get back to the shop.

00:56:34.820 --> 00:56:39.347
The oldy Shakespeare tea shopping in wire piddle.

00:56:39.407 --> 00:56:43.980
Traditional English tea shop with traditional homemade cakes.

00:56:44.040 --> 00:56:47.712
Here's a leaflet for you, sir, and your lovely daughter.

00:56:47.772 --> 00:56:48.763
Thank you.

00:56:48.823 --> 00:56:52.125
Hope to see both of you in our tea shop.

00:56:52.186 --> 00:57:04.012
The only Shakespeare tea-shroppy in wire-piddle has Shakespeare sent himself on his deathbed, never in the field of human conflict.

00:57:04.052 --> 00:57:06.003
Winston Churchill said that as well.

00:57:06.063 --> 00:57:07.594
It wasn't Shakespeare.

00:57:07.654 --> 00:57:09.004
Leave me alone.

00:57:09.064 --> 00:57:12.926
Go to speaker's corner and annoy some people there.

00:57:12.986 --> 00:57:15.028
What a terrible woman.

00:57:15.088 --> 00:57:17.709
She has no idea at all.

00:57:17.769 --> 00:57:20.801
I can't believe it.

00:57:20.861 --> 00:57:24.276
A special visit.

00:57:24.336 --> 00:57:27.011
Never again.

00:57:27.071 --> 00:57:28.792
What's the matter now?

00:57:28.852 --> 00:57:31.274
Everywhere I went, she was there.

00:57:31.334 --> 00:57:33.786
Called herself an historian.

00:57:33.846 --> 00:57:35.267
She had no idea.

00:57:35.327 --> 00:57:36.888
No idea at all.

00:57:36.948 --> 00:57:37.919
Yes, dear.

00:57:37.979 --> 00:57:42.002
You've told me the same story at least ten times since breakfast.

00:57:42.022 --> 00:57:43.143
Yes, I mean.

00:57:43.183 --> 00:57:44.584
You can't complain.

00:57:44.604 --> 00:57:48.416
I think we've had at least 20 new customers so far.

00:57:48.476 --> 00:57:49.528
Excuse me.

00:57:49.588 --> 00:57:50.578
You over there.

00:57:50.638 --> 00:57:51.809
Come here, please.

00:57:51.869 --> 00:57:52.950
Are you referring to me?

00:57:52.970 --> 00:57:54.040
Yes, you.

00:57:54.100 --> 00:57:56.741
I thought slavery had been abolished.

00:57:56.802 --> 00:57:59.934
I'm sorry to be more polite to the customers, Pete.

00:57:59.994 --> 00:58:00.944
Yes, dear.

00:58:01.004 --> 00:58:02.565
Yes, dear.

00:58:02.626 --> 00:58:04.461
How can I help you, madam?

00:58:04.521 --> 00:58:06.485
This is not a proper scone.

00:58:06.545 --> 00:58:07.726
What are these?

00:58:07.786 --> 00:58:12.827
I believe in the catering world, they are usually called sultanas, madam.

00:58:12.887 --> 00:58:14.937
Maybe you haven't heard of them before.

00:58:14.997 --> 00:58:16.818
Don't be so stupid.

00:58:16.878 --> 00:58:21.739
I want you to take this away and bring me another scone without sultanas.

00:58:21.799 --> 00:58:28.040
Of course, madam, whatever you say, would you also like the strawberries removed from the strawberry jam?

00:58:28.100 --> 00:58:29.080
No, thank you.

00:58:29.140 --> 00:58:30.320
The jam is fine.

00:58:30.380 --> 00:58:33.255
Well, I'm pleased to hear that.

00:58:33.315 --> 00:58:36.570
Sure, the people we get in here.

00:58:36.630 --> 00:58:38.440
What's the matter now?

00:58:38.500 --> 00:58:42.831
Queen Victoria at Table Six wants a scone without sultanas.

00:58:42.891 --> 00:58:44.692
I'm just making some more.

00:58:44.752 --> 00:58:46.972
I can make some without sultanas.

00:58:47.032 --> 00:58:48.083
Thank you, Mum.

00:58:48.143 --> 00:58:50.973
You see, Pete, you shouldn't get so stressed.

00:58:51.034 --> 00:58:52.274
I'm not stressed!

00:58:52.334 --> 00:58:55.134
It's just the people we get in here.

00:58:55.194 --> 00:58:56.545
What are you doing?

00:58:56.605 --> 00:58:58.525
Don't put a knife in the mix of Pete!

00:58:58.586 --> 00:59:00.266
I just want to try the...

00:59:00.286 --> 00:59:04.698
Bloody hell!

00:59:04.758 --> 00:59:07.732
Why did you turn the mixer on?

00:59:07.792 --> 00:59:09.345
I didn't see you!

00:59:09.405 --> 00:59:11.399
Get out of the kitchen, Pete!

00:59:11.459 --> 00:59:12.321
God!

00:59:12.381 --> 00:59:13.752
I'm going!

00:59:13.812 --> 00:59:15.604
Good morning, sir.

00:59:15.664 --> 00:59:20.288
I'm a local health inspector, and I want to inspect your premises, please.

00:59:20.348 --> 00:59:21.549
Yes, and I'm...

00:59:21.569 --> 00:59:25.043
I'm still waiting for my scone without saltanas.

00:59:25.103 --> 00:59:26.674
Just be quiet and wait.

00:59:26.734 --> 00:59:29.366
Do you always speak to your customers like that?

00:59:29.426 --> 00:59:29.907
No.

00:59:29.967 --> 00:59:32.940
Only the ones I would like deported to the continent.

00:59:33.000 --> 00:59:34.731
Now, who did you say you were?

00:59:34.791 --> 00:59:36.563
The local health inspector.

00:59:36.623 --> 00:59:38.715
Here to check your kitchen.

00:59:38.775 --> 00:59:41.173
Yes, I'm wonderful.

00:59:41.233 --> 00:59:44.371
Ah yes, that's all we need.

00:59:44.432 --> 00:59:46.684
Are you feeling alright?

00:59:46.744 --> 00:59:50.507
Ah yes, lovely day isn't it?

00:59:50.567 --> 00:59:53.780
Pity about the cricket score.

00:59:53.840 --> 00:59:55.982
Please just wait there.

00:59:56.042 --> 00:59:57.813
Meet to nice you.

00:59:57.874 --> 01:00:01.157
I mean everyone.

01:00:01.217 --> 01:00:04.540
What are you talking about?

01:00:04.600 --> 01:00:05.783
Careful where you walk.

01:00:05.843 --> 01:00:06.464
Not there.

01:00:06.524 --> 01:00:07.555
He's here.

01:00:07.615 --> 01:00:09.237
Now look what you've done.

01:00:09.298 --> 01:00:10.780
The floor is slippery.

01:00:10.840 --> 01:00:12.022
Be careful.

01:00:12.082 --> 01:00:13.254
He's here.

01:00:13.314 --> 01:00:15.036
The man who phoned.

01:00:15.096 --> 01:00:17.560
Each man who phoned.

01:00:17.620 --> 01:00:18.971
Oh no!

01:00:19.031 --> 01:00:20.602
What the hell, Inspector?

01:00:20.662 --> 01:00:21.292
Yes!

01:00:21.352 --> 01:00:22.523
Don't let him come in here.

01:00:22.583 --> 01:00:23.703
Look at this mess.

01:00:23.743 --> 01:00:24.564
Give him in the shop.

01:00:24.624 --> 01:00:25.414
How?

01:00:25.474 --> 01:00:27.225
Use your imagination.

01:00:27.245 --> 01:00:28.946
Sing songs.

01:00:29.006 --> 01:00:30.547
Sing songs?

01:00:30.607 --> 01:00:32.688
Sing songs to him?

01:00:32.748 --> 01:00:34.789
Are you completely mad?

01:00:34.849 --> 01:00:36.040
What do you suggest?

01:00:36.100 --> 01:00:37.510
Handles Messiah.

01:00:37.570 --> 01:00:40.602
Most songs, Requiem, might be more appropriate.

01:00:40.662 --> 01:00:46.626
Just do something, but get out of here.

01:00:46.686 --> 01:00:49.677
Hello?

01:00:49.697 --> 01:00:52.038
Where's he gone?

01:00:52.098 --> 01:00:53.558
Where's he gone?

01:00:53.618 --> 01:00:59.289
If you are referring to that gentleman, he's gone to inspect the sanitary arrangements.

01:00:59.349 --> 01:01:02.099
Just had a look at your toilets.

01:01:02.159 --> 01:01:06.440
I'm afraid it is forbidden to have outside toilets like those in a restaurant.

01:01:06.500 --> 01:01:07.670
But I...

01:01:07.730 --> 01:01:10.361
I want to inspect your kitchen now, please.

01:01:10.421 --> 01:01:13.321
Maybe you would like a cup of tea first.

01:01:13.381 --> 01:01:14.411
No, thank you.

01:01:14.471 --> 01:01:15.962
Just your kitchen, please.

01:01:16.022 --> 01:01:18.072
I know we can...

01:01:18.132 --> 01:01:21.653
Look at the flowers in the garden.

01:01:21.713 --> 01:01:23.414
Do you like flowers?

01:01:23.474 --> 01:01:25.694
Just the kitchen, please.

01:01:25.754 --> 01:01:27.525
It's through this door, isn't it?

01:01:27.585 --> 01:01:34.137
Maybe you would like to hear me sing?

01:01:34.197 --> 01:01:35.728
I beg your pardon?

01:01:35.788 --> 01:01:36.888
Stupid idea.

01:01:36.948 --> 01:01:39.954
It wasn't my idea.

01:01:40.014 --> 01:01:44.382
Good morning madam.

01:01:44.442 --> 01:01:46.494
Oh, let me help you up.

01:01:46.554 --> 01:01:48.025
Sorry about that.

01:01:48.045 --> 01:01:51.378
Perhaps you would like a scone.

01:01:51.438 --> 01:01:53.640
We're all without sultanas.

01:01:53.700 --> 01:01:55.132
No, thank you.

01:01:55.192 --> 01:01:57.053
What a mess in here.

01:01:57.113 --> 01:01:59.004
It's not acceptable.

01:01:59.064 --> 01:02:01.046
And what's that noise?

01:02:01.106 --> 01:02:03.218
It's coming from the cupboard over there.

01:02:03.278 --> 01:02:05.870
Let me see.

01:02:05.930 --> 01:02:10.203
There's a rat in a box in here!

01:02:10.263 --> 01:02:11.564
I'll kill her!

01:02:11.624 --> 01:02:12.976
Where's Josephine?

01:02:13.036 --> 01:02:14.166
Where is he?

01:02:14.226 --> 01:02:16.328
You must close this shop now.

01:02:16.388 --> 01:02:18.821
It's an absolute disgrace.

01:02:18.881 --> 01:02:23.305
I'll be writing a full report and you can expect a very heavy pine.

01:02:23.365 --> 01:02:25.256
Oh no!

01:02:25.317 --> 01:02:27.652
It's the end!

01:02:27.712 --> 01:02:30.488
What's going on?

01:02:30.548 --> 01:02:32.070
Stop it Pete.

01:02:32.130 --> 01:02:33.360
Don't be stupid.

01:02:33.420 --> 01:02:34.732
Come here Josephine.

01:02:34.792 --> 01:02:36.222
What's happening here?

01:02:36.282 --> 01:02:37.994
Are you all going bonkers?

01:02:38.054 --> 01:02:39.145
And who are you?

01:02:39.205 --> 01:02:43.188
I'm the local health inspector and I'm closing this shop now.

01:02:43.248 --> 01:02:44.499
You can't be.

01:02:44.559 --> 01:02:45.820
It was a joke.

01:02:45.880 --> 01:02:48.302
It's not a joke Josephine.

01:02:48.362 --> 01:02:50.594
He phoned a few days ago.

01:02:50.654 --> 01:02:52.255
That was me.

01:02:52.315 --> 01:02:54.607
I pretended to be the health inspector.

01:02:54.667 --> 01:02:55.988
It was a joke.

01:02:56.048 --> 01:02:57.429
What's happening here?

01:02:57.489 --> 01:02:59.230
What are you talking about?

01:02:59.290 --> 01:03:01.332
Ask Gran, she knows.

01:03:01.392 --> 01:03:04.864
Yes, I was there when Josie phoned you.

01:03:04.924 --> 01:03:07.256
She's very clever, isn't she?

01:03:07.316 --> 01:03:13.701
She should become an actress, maybe a famous Hollywood actress in the films.

01:03:13.721 --> 01:03:16.612
But who are you?

01:03:16.672 --> 01:03:19.755
Have you asked him to show you his credentials?

01:03:19.815 --> 01:03:21.686
Ah, I must go now.

01:03:21.746 --> 01:03:23.558
I have another key shot to visit.

01:03:23.618 --> 01:03:25.674
Don't forget what I said.

01:03:25.734 --> 01:03:28.740
Oh hello Trevor, what a surprise.

01:03:28.800 --> 01:03:30.901
Haven't seen you for a long time.

01:03:30.961 --> 01:03:31.801
How are you?

01:03:31.821 --> 01:03:33.672
Oh, hello.

01:03:33.732 --> 01:03:34.452
Sorry.

01:03:34.513 --> 01:03:35.583
Yes, I'm fine.

01:03:35.643 --> 01:03:36.714
Thanks.

01:03:36.774 --> 01:03:39.064
Goodbye.

01:03:39.124 --> 01:03:42.026
Strange man.

01:03:42.086 --> 01:03:43.196
What's all the noise?

01:03:43.256 --> 01:03:44.937
Julian and I were worried.

01:03:44.997 --> 01:03:46.518
What's happening in here?

01:03:46.578 --> 01:03:48.639
It's a terrible mess.

01:03:48.699 --> 01:03:51.235
The health inspector was here.

01:03:51.295 --> 01:03:52.260
When?

01:03:52.320 --> 01:03:53.281
Today?

01:03:53.342 --> 01:03:55.624
When you came in you said hello.

01:03:55.684 --> 01:03:57.055
You must know him.

01:03:57.115 --> 01:03:59.407
Oh yes, that's Trevor McMubble.

01:03:59.427 --> 01:04:01.350
He's an old school friend.

01:04:01.410 --> 01:04:03.412
I sometimes see him in Stratford.

01:04:03.472 --> 01:04:05.425
He ends a tee shot there.

01:04:05.485 --> 01:04:08.098
He owns a t-shot.

01:04:08.158 --> 01:04:10.050
But I don't understand.

01:04:10.110 --> 01:04:13.172
Why did he say he was a health inspector?

01:04:13.232 --> 01:04:16.764
I think I'm beginning to understand.

01:04:16.824 --> 01:04:20.356
He's worried about the competition from our t-shot.

01:04:20.417 --> 01:04:24.419
Since I did my advertising in Stratford, we've had more customers.

01:04:24.479 --> 01:04:28.836
He wanted to frighten us and get us to close down.

01:04:28.896 --> 01:04:32.133
What a shock that was.

01:04:32.193 --> 01:04:35.236
What a horrible thing to do.

01:04:35.296 --> 01:04:37.838
Thank God, that's over and done with.

01:04:37.898 --> 01:04:41.162
Come on everybody, let's have a nice cup of tea.

01:04:41.222 --> 01:04:42.333
Grand?

01:04:42.394 --> 01:04:46.514
I even like your scones without sultanas.

01:04:46.574 --> 01:04:48.902
Good afternoon.

01:04:48.962 --> 01:04:51.425
I'm from the Stratford Public Health Office.

01:04:51.485 --> 01:04:55.308
I've had reports that there are rats in this tee shop.

01:04:55.368 --> 01:04:59.052
Is this another friend of yours, Si?

01:04:59.112 --> 01:04:59.953
Sorry, love.

01:05:00.013 --> 01:05:00.804
You're too late.

01:05:00.864 --> 01:05:03.426
No, your friend was here one minute ago.

01:05:03.486 --> 01:05:04.808
We're not worried.

01:05:04.868 --> 01:05:06.129
But this is serious.

01:05:06.149 --> 01:05:09.282
If the reports are correct, I'll have to close this shop.

01:05:09.342 --> 01:05:11.073
I want to have a look around.

01:05:11.133 --> 01:05:12.795
Of course you do, madam.

01:05:12.855 --> 01:05:16.077
But you can't get out of our shop now.

01:05:16.137 --> 01:05:17.388
We know your game.

01:05:17.448 --> 01:05:18.219
But what?

01:05:18.279 --> 01:05:19.140
Go on out.

01:05:19.200 --> 01:05:20.180
We don't believe you.

01:05:20.240 --> 01:05:21.521
You don't frighten us.

01:05:21.541 --> 01:05:22.182
Stop it.

01:05:22.222 --> 01:05:23.162
What are you doing?

01:05:23.222 --> 01:05:24.794
Here's my identity card.

01:05:24.854 --> 01:05:27.186
Yes, of course it is.

01:05:27.246 --> 01:05:29.688
You have an identity card.

01:05:29.748 --> 01:05:31.559
What a surprise.

01:05:31.619 --> 01:05:33.310
Your partner in Grinden.

01:05:33.330 --> 01:05:36.956
Let me see it.

01:05:37.016 --> 01:05:40.262
Yes.

01:05:40.322 --> 01:05:44.444
I think you should look at this identity card.

01:05:44.504 --> 01:05:46.775
It's genuine.

01:05:46.835 --> 01:05:48.066
What?

01:05:48.126 --> 01:05:49.226
It can't be.

01:05:49.286 --> 01:05:53.558
Let me look.

01:05:53.618 --> 01:05:54.588
Oh no.

